  "lessons": [
    {
      "key": "Pre-Assessment",
      "name": "Pre-Assessment",
      "absolute_position": 1,
      "lockable": true,
      "has_lesson_plan": false,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "assessment": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Pre-Assessment",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "ai-generated-design-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 1: Introduction to Web Apps",
      "name": "Lesson 1: Welcome to Web Apps",
      "absolute_position": 2,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How do web apps shape our daily lives?**\n\nIn this lesson, students build and test a paper prototype of a web app, then compare their designs with classmates. What they discover is the fun part: without any instructions, most groups make surprisingly similar choices about where to put things. That's the lesson's central insight — layout patterns aren't random, they're intentional design decisions that designers make to guide how users think and move. Students leave with names for the structural components they've been using all along and an early sense that they've already been thinking like designers.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Paper App Prototype Activity Guides (1 per student)\n- Print and cut mystery component sets (1 per pair)\n- Print copies of the Unit Guides (1 per student)\n- Provide tape or glue (1 per pair)",
        "student_overview": "**How do web apps shape our daily lives?**\n\nHave you ever opened a brand new app and immediately knew where to look for the menu? Or tapped exactly where you expected a button to be? Today you're going to find out why by building your own app out of paper. You'll design a layout, test it with a classmate, and probably discover something surprising along the way: the choices you make on your own look a lot like the apps you already love. That's not a coincidence. That's design."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 1: Introduction to Web Apps",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-generated-design-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 2: The User's Experience",
      "name": "Lesson 2: The User's Experience",
      "absolute_position": 3,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**Why do some web apps feel easy and others frustrating?**\n\nThe lesson opens with a comparison that usually sparks strong reactions: Amazon in 2000 versus Amazon in 2025. Students see the same website two decades apart and immediately have opinions — which is the point. From there, they step into the role of UX Designers tasked with evaluating a pet adoption app from the perspective of a specific user profile. The move from \"I personally find this confusing\" to \"this doesn't work for my user because...\" is the central skill this lesson builds. It's the first time students practice separating their own preferences from their user's needs, which is harder than it sounds and more important than almost anything else in the unit.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the The User's Experience activity guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**Why do some web apps feel easy and others frustrating?**\n\nWould you rather use Amazon's website from 2000 or from 2025? The answer seems obvious — but have you ever thought about why? Today you'll dig into exactly that. You'll step into the role of a UX Designer evaluating a real app, but here's the twist: you're not evaluating it for yourself. You're evaluating it for a specific user with specific needs. What you think of the design doesn't matter. What your user experiences does."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 2: The User's Experience",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-generated-design-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 3: Build Your First Web Page with AI",
      "name": "Lesson 3: Build Your First Web Page with AI",
      "absolute_position": 4,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can AI help us turn our ideas into a web page?**\n\nBefore students generate anything, they make predictions: given a prompt for a gourmet pizza restaurant website, what sections will probably appear? Then they see the AI's output and compare it to what they guessed. That prediction moment matters — it gets students thinking about AI as a pattern-matching system rather than a magic box, which is exactly the framing the lesson then formalizes. Students learn how AI uses training data to recognize patterns and generate predictions, get introduced to prompt engineering, and see for the first time that their HTML and CSS live in two separate files. The gap between a vague prompt and a specific one is made concrete through side-by-side comparisons before students try it themselves.",
        "student_overview": "**How can AI help us turn our ideas into a web page?**\n\nBefore you build anything today, you're going to make a prediction. If you gave AI this prompt — \"Build a modern landing page for a gourmet pizza restaurant with a red and charcoal color palette\" — what do you think would show up? Make your guess, then find out if you were right. What you discover will tell you a lot about how AI actually works: not magic, not random, but pattern recognition. That's the foundation everything else in this unit is built on."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 3: Build Your First Web Page with AI",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-generated-design-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 4: Finding Your Style",
      "name": "Lesson 4: Finding Your Style",
      "absolute_position": 5,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can we draw inspiration from existing designs to guide our own?**\n\nStudents open this lesson by writing one first-impression word for two different websites and guessing who each one is designed for. The exercise is fast and intuitive — and it reveals something useful: students already have strong aesthetic instincts, they just don't have language for them yet. The lesson builds on that by having students critique real websites for strengths and weaknesses, then channel what they've noticed into building their own mood board. The peer feedback at the end — structured around \"one element that communicates your style well\" and \"one way to strengthen your aesthetic direction\" — introduces the professional design review format students will use throughout the unit.",
        "student_overview": "**How can we draw inspiration from existing designs to guide our own?**\n\nLook at a website for three seconds. What's your gut reaction? One word. That instinct you have — the immediate sense of whether something feels right — is something designers work hard to create. Today you're going to figure out how they do it, by studying real sites, identifying what works and what doesn't, and then making those same choices for your own project. You'll build a mood board: your visual direction before you've committed to anything. Think of it as finding your style before you build it."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 4: Finding Your Style",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-generated-design-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 5: Brand Identity",
      "name": "Lesson 5: Brand Identity",
      "absolute_position": 6,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**How do fonts and colors tell a story?**\n\nThe lesson opens with four color palettes and a simple challenge: label each one with a mood — Energetic, Calming, Playful, or Luxury. Then students see the brands those palettes belong to and discover whether the colors actually fit. It's a quick exercise that makes the stakes of brand decisions tangible before students make their own. From there, students work as Brand Designers — their client needs a brand kit, and every choice has to be justified against their target user. The lesson includes two substantive ethics moments: a discussion about which audiences might feel left out by certain design choices, and a wrap-up question about whose visual preferences AI defaults to when generating design suggestions.",
        "student_overview": "**How do fonts and colors tell a story?**\n\nWhat mood does this color palette give you? Energetic? Luxurious? Playful? Today you'll start by matching palettes to moods — and then find out which brands they actually belong to. Color is doing real work in those decisions, and so is typography. In this lesson, you're stepping into the role of a Brand Designer with a real brief: your client needs a brand kit, and you have to be able to explain every choice you make. Why this color? Why this font? What does it say to your user — and what might it accidentally say to someone you didn't think about?"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 5: Brand Identity",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-generated-design-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 6: Intro to CSS",
      "name": "Lesson 6: Intro to CSS",
      "absolute_position": 7,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**How do we make our web app reflect our brand identity?**\n\nThe warm-up does something visually striking: students sketch what a styled webpage would look like without any CSS, then annotate what stays the same and what disappears. It makes the separation between HTML and CSS immediately concrete — structure stays, style vanishes. From there, students are introduced to the Front-End Developer role and the prompt.md file: a structured planning tool with three questions (goals, constraints, edge cases) that they'll use to write better prompts before generating their stylesheet. Side-by-side prompt comparisons make the quality difference explicit before students try it themselves. The lesson ends with students applying CSS to their own web app and reflecting on what the AI changed and why.",
        "student_overview": "**How do we make our web app reflect our brand identity?**\n\nWhat would your favorite website look like if all the styling suddenly disappeared? Same structure, same content — but no colors, no fonts, no spacing. Just raw HTML. Today you'll actually sketch that out, and then you'll learn how to bring the style back — by writing better prompts. You'll meet the prompt.md file: a planning tool that helps you tell AI exactly what you want before you ask for it. The difference between a vague CSS prompt and a specific one is bigger than you'd expect. Today you'll see exactly why."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 6: Intro to CSS",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-generated-design-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 7: Meet Your User",
      "name": "Lesson 7: Meet Your User",
      "absolute_position": 8,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How do we define our user’s needs clearly?**\n\nThe warm-up sets the tone: \"Think of a time when technology didn't meet your needs. In one word, how did it make you feel.\" That frustration is the whole lesson in miniature — it's what happens when designers don't start with a real person. Students are then walked through a worked example of building a persona and problem statement from scratch, step by step: who is this person, what do they say and think and feel and do, what's their frustration, what's their goal, and finally, what's the problem statement. The contrast between a strong and weak problem statement is shown explicitly, so students understand that \"Jordan needs an app that sends notifications\" is not the same as \"Jordan needs a way to see all club meeting updates in one place because juggling multiple group chats makes them miss important information.\"",
        "student_overview": "**How do we define our user’s needs clearly?**\n\nThink of a time when an app or website just didn't work for you. How did that feel? That frustration is exactly what good designers try to prevent — by starting with a real person before they build anything. Today that's what you're doing. You'll build a persona: a detailed picture of your user's life, needs, and frustrations. Then you'll write a problem statement that captures exactly what you're trying to solve for them. Fair warning: the first version is almost never specific enough. That's part of the lesson."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 7: Meet Your User",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-generated-design-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 8: Site Maps",
      "name": "Lesson 8: Site Maps",
      "absolute_position": 9,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**How do we break down a site into parts?**\n\nThe warm-up is a navigation puzzle: students follow paths through a site map to complete two tasks — buying pants and finding return information — then discuss what those paths reveal about how the site is organized. It's a fast, concrete way to show that site structure is a design decision with real consequences for users. From there, students take on the UX Designer role with a client brief: map out how the pages connect before any building starts. A physical card-sorting activity has students arrange website sections and draw connections between them, then the class compares results. The lesson closes with students generating a site from their map using AI, which makes the stakes of a clear versus vague structure immediately visible.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Website Cards (one set for each pair of students)",
        "student_overview": "**How do we break down a site into parts?**\n\nYou want to buy pants on a website. You start at the Home page. Where do you go next? If the site is organized well, you find what you need in a few clicks. If it isn't, you wander. Today you're going to make sure your app doesn't make users wander. You'll build a site map — a blueprint that shows what pages your app has and how they connect — and then hand it to AI to generate the actual structure. How clear your map is determines how well AI understands what to build. That's on you."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 8: Site Maps",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-generated-design-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 9: Accessibility in Design",
      "name": "Lesson 9: Accessibility in Design",
      "absolute_position": 10,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**How can we make sites usable for everyone?**\n\nThe lesson opens with a before-and-after comparison of two pizza ordering websites — one confusing, one improved — and asks students to notice what changed and what could go wrong if no one fixed the first version. That \"what could go wrong\" question is the lesson's moral center: accessibility failures aren't just aesthetic problems, they're barriers that determine whether someone can participate at all. Students work as Inclusive Designers, starting with the striking statistic that nearly 95% of websites have accessibility issues, running audits on Yale School of Art, MIT Media Lab, and NASA, and then learning that AI reproduces these patterns by default because it learns from the web that already exists. The POUR framework gives students a professional evaluation tool they apply to real-world outputs.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Accessibility and Design activity guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**How can we make sites usable for everyone?**\n\nNearly 95% of websites have accessibility issues. That's not a small problem — that's most of the internet failing a significant portion of its users. Today you're going to see that up close. You'll audit three well-known websites — Yale School of Art, MIT Media Lab, and NASA — using a professional framework called POUR, and you'll find real problems on real sites. Then you'll connect what you found to AI: where do those problems come from, and what does it mean that AI learned from websites like these?"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 9: Accessibility in Design",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-generated-design-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 10: Wireframes",
      "name": "Lesson 10: Wireframes",
      "absolute_position": 11,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**Why sketch before building?**\n\nThe lesson opens with a comparison: which is easier to change, a detailed polished design or a rough structural sketch? The answer sets up everything that follows. Students do two rapid sketch sprints — a home page, then an about page — for the same fictional music playlist app, comparing their Round 1 and Round 2 sketches to notice how using formal wireframe components makes layouts clearer and more structured. The lesson formally introduces abstraction alongside wireframing: when you strip out colors and details and keep only structure, that's not a limitation, it's a thinking skill. Students then wireframe their own app in Sketch Lab and give and receive peer feedback using the \"I like / I wonder / I wish\" format.",
        "student_overview": "**Why sketch before building?**\n\nWhich is easier to change: a finished, polished design or a rough sketch made of boxes and lines? Today you'll find out why designers always start with the sketch. You'll do two quick one-minute sprints to wireframe a music playlist app — first instinctively, then using a set of standard components — and you'll probably notice your second version is clearer than your first. Then you'll apply that same thinking to your own app. What you wireframe today is what you'll hand to AI next lesson, so clarity matters."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 10: Wireframes",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-generated-design-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 11: From Wireframes to Layouts",
      "name": "Lesson 11: From Wireframes to Layouts",
      "absolute_position": 12,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How do we turn sketches into working layouts with AI?**\n\nThe lesson's warm-up poses a simple question: would you rather have a map, written directions, or both? The answer is both — and that's exactly what students give AI in this lesson: a visual wireframe and a written description, together. Students see how AI interprets wireframes by detecting spacing, labels, grouping, and alignment, and why it gets things wrong when any of those are ambiguous. The lesson's central activity is the gap between what students intended their wireframe to communicate and what AI actually produced — and what they need to change in their prompt to close that gap. That iteration loop is the lesson's most important learning moment.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the From Wireframes to Layouts activity guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**How do we turn sketches into working layouts with AI?**\n\nWould you rather navigate somewhere with a map, written directions, or both? Both, right? Today you're going to give AI both: your wireframe for the visual layout and a written prompt for the context and content. Then you'll see what it does with them — and it might not be exactly what you expected. Your job is to figure out why AI interpreted your design the way it did and what you need to change to get it closer to what you actually had in mind. The clearer your wireframe and prompt, the closer the output will be. This is where planning pays off."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 11: From Wireframes to Layouts",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-generated-design-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 12: Designing for Responsiveness",
      "name": "Lesson 12: Designing for Responsiveness",
      "absolute_position": 13,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**How should layouts adapt across devices?**\n\nThe warm-up is a video of a website being resized from desktop to tablet to phone, with the class asked to identify what changes and what breaks. It's immediately concrete: students watch something familiar fall apart. From there, students learn that over 65% of web traffic comes from mobile devices and that Google ranks sites based on mobile performance — which reframes responsiveness from a nice-to-have to a professional requirement. Students take on the Software Test Engineer role, introduced with a real-world video, and run their own AI-generated layouts through a responsiveness audit as a professional deliverable: document the issues, fix them, report what changed. The lesson closes with a question about who gets excluded when sites only work on desktops.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Designing for Responsiveness activity guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**How should layouts adapt across devices?**\n\nWatch what happens to this website as the screen gets smaller. Some things adapt. Some things break. Most people never notice — until they're the one using a phone. Today you will notice, because it's your job to notice. You're stepping into the role of a Software Test Engineer, and your assignment is a bug report: this layout doesn't work on mobile. Find the problems, fix them, and document what you changed. Then think about who gets left out when a site only works on a desktop. That question matters more than you might expect."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 12: Designing for Responsiveness",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "ai-generated-design-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 13: Digital Magazine Project",
      "name": "Lesson 13: Digital Magazine Project",
      "absolute_position": 14,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "assessment": true,
        "overview": "**How can we bring our design plan to life in a way that meets our user’s needs?**\n\nThe project spans three days with a clear milestone structure — Plan, Build, Finalize — and ends with a Gallery Walk where students move around the room leaving sticky-note feedback on each other's work. Day 1 is define and plan: students use everything from the unit (brand kit, site map, wireframes) to plan their magazine before touching the AI tools. Day 2 is build: students generate their first draft and participate in a TAG peer review (Tell, Ask, Give). Day 3 is finalize: students self-assess against the rubric, make final adjustments, and showcase their work. Each day ends with a discussion prompt that closes the loop on the HCD thread running through the unit — Day 1 asks who the magazine is for, Day 2 asks what students did when AI surprised them, and Day 3 asks whether the final product actually serves the user they defined on Day 1.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Digital Magazine Project Guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**How can we bring our design plan to life in a way that meets our user’s needs?**\n\nEverything in this unit has been building toward this. Your brand kit, your user persona, your site map, your wireframes, your accessibility thinking — you're going to use all of it to design and build a complete digital magazine over the next three days. A digital magazine is a multi-page web app that works like a real publication: a defined theme, a consistent visual identity, content organized for a specific reader. You'll plan it, build it with AI, refine it based on feedback, and present it in a Gallery Walk. When you're done, you'll have something real — something you built, something you can explain, and something you can put in your portfolio."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 13: Digital Magazine Project",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-4",
        "script.name": "ai-generated-design-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "End of Unit Assessment",
      "name": "End of Unit Assessment",
      "absolute_position": 15,
      "lockable": true,
