  ],
  "activity_sections": [
    {
      "key": "8ec57063-d9db-43b8-beef-8053871447a4",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Pre-Assessment"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8ec57063-d9db-43b8-beef-8053871447a4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "12cbdb20-258c-445c-a1b1-45a6968794c7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4aa92abe-6e2f-479e-afe7-317589295b8f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** The goal is to help students recognize that Web Apps are interactive systems defined by their structure and response, regardless of how they are accessed. It shifts their focus from \"software icons\" to the design patterns (like menus and buttons) that make those apps usable across different devices.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students a moment to jot down their responses. Have students share with a partner and then call on a few volunteers to share with the class. As students respond, write key verbs on the board (e.g., message, buy, upload, search, post, edit).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for students mentioning specific actions like \"ordering food,\" \"checking grades,\" or messaging friends\" rather than app names. If students get stuck, prompt with: \"Could you still search? Still message? Still buy something?\"",
        "name": "The App-Less Day"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4aa92abe-6e2f-479e-afe7-317589295b8f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "77f2e3b0-a9b5-48fe-8c4f-45254dc374de"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b706a40d-79da-435e-90dc-54d87dcc0f3f",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Notice that even without installing an app, you still expect to message, search, post, or buy. That's because what matters isn't the icon, it's the interaction.",
        "remarks": true,
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students focus on the app itself, redirect with: \"What are you actually doing in that app?\" or \"What action are you trying to complete.\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b706a40d-79da-435e-90dc-54d87dcc0f3f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "77f2e3b0-a9b5-48fe-8c4f-45254dc374de"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "111df894-22f6-474c-b3fa-159a96af3868",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "111df894-22f6-474c-b3fa-159a96af3868",
        "lesson_activity.key": "77f2e3b0-a9b5-48fe-8c4f-45254dc374de"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d42b80f7-5d66-4545-890e-20cb242f331a",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the AI-Generated Design Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *web app*. Emphasize the word \"respond\" and connect it to the verbs students just shared."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d42b80f7-5d66-4545-890e-20cb242f331a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "77f2e3b0-a9b5-48fe-8c4f-45254dc374de"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a7ca34b0-73c5-4623-9ac4-29b354078d34",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Notice you didn't say \"open Instagram.\" You said things like message, search, and post. A web app isn't just information. It responds to your actions. Today, we're going to look at how those responses are intentionally designed and how web apps affect our lives.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a7ca34b0-73c5-4623-9ac4-29b354078d34",
        "lesson_activity.key": "77f2e3b0-a9b5-48fe-8c4f-45254dc374de"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1bfb6797-5ac3-4a50-818c-86296cc6da89",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1bfb6797-5ac3-4a50-818c-86296cc6da89",
        "lesson_activity.key": "77f2e3b0-a9b5-48fe-8c4f-45254dc374de"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9e650b65-8d8d-4c73-b026-ce34d8726441",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Help students discover that web pages are intentionally structured and that visible parts work together to support user interaction.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Put students into groups of two.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each pair a set of the mystery components and a blank sheet of paper.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students choose one app purpose.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students arrange the mystery components on a blank sheet of paper to create an app layout. They should:\n1. Arrange all components on their paper.\n1. Write on the components to show what they are – for example, if a component has a button, what is the name of that button? If there is a heading, what does it say?\n1. For every button or interactive element, draw an arrow and write what the app does in response – for example, opens the menu or shows a dog's bio in a pop-up window.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Pair each group with another pair. Instruct one group to act as users while the other acts as their app.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for whether students are thinking in actions and responses. Listen for moments where a tester says, \"What happens when I click this?\" If an app has no clear response, prompt designers to add one. Encourage students to think in terms of: User action → App response.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Paper App Prototype",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If groups finish early, ask: \"Did the app actually help you complete the goal?\", \"Where did you get stuck?\", or \"What did you expect to happen that didn't?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9e650b65-8d8d-4c73-b026-ce34d8726441",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5d9dcce1-b6ee-4c6a-a93b-5431f75f936f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0c46e0fa-cbb9-4a79-b9ff-8b62046f6b94",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0c46e0fa-cbb9-4a79-b9ff-8b62046f6b94",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5d9dcce1-b6ee-4c6a-a93b-5431f75f936f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "670500a4-dc31-4503-a707-284808e62a1b",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have pairs first meet with a group that chose the same app and discuss the questions in their activity guide. Then have them meet with a group that chose a different app and discuss the same questions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As you walk around, listen for students noticing similar component placement, especially elements placed at the top or grouped together. Pay attention to moments where a user taps something and the app doesn't respond. If groups get stuck, prompt them by asking what surprised them, whether they noticed anything showing up in similar locations, or what happened when someone tried to use the layout.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time is limited, prioritize the same-app comparison — that's where pattern recognition is strongest and the \"design is intentional\" insight usually emerges."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "670500a4-dc31-4503-a707-284808e62a1b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5d9dcce1-b6ee-4c6a-a93b-5431f75f936f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a5e7224e-77a8-4183-b896-8b9bf199af9c",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a5e7224e-77a8-4183-b896-8b9bf199af9c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5d9dcce1-b6ee-4c6a-a93b-5431f75f936f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9916b587-4879-4ec2-b7ff-a38912523321",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have pairs meet with a group that chose a different app and discuss the same questions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What did you notice about where most groups placed their components?*\n- *Why do you think so many layouts looked similar?*\n- *When testing, what happened if a user clicked something the app hadn't planned for?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to recognize that their layouts were not random. Even without instructions, many groups placed components in similar locations and expected certain parts to respond when clicked. Students should begin to realize that web app structure follows shared design patterns and that interaction only works when those parts are intentionally planned."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9916b587-4879-4ec2-b7ff-a38912523321",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5d9dcce1-b6ee-4c6a-a93b-5431f75f936f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a77d64d6-4cf2-4844-ba4d-4e21282c6cfd",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Introduce pattern recognition and common components that make up web apps.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Common App Components"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a77d64d6-4cf2-4844-ba4d-4e21282c6cfd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5d9dcce1-b6ee-4c6a-a93b-5431f75f936f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ad853fa4-ab72-48c8-85b7-405e2b3285ca",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "What you just noticed has a name. In design and in computer science, we call this pattern recognition — spotting recurring structures that tell us something about how a system works. You weren't given instructions about where to put the navigation bar. You just knew. That's because you've seen it in the same place hundreds of times, and your brain learned the pattern. Designers use this skill when they study what works across different sites. And AI uses it too — it learns patterns from data the same way you learn patterns from experience. We'll come back to this idea throughout the unit.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ad853fa4-ab72-48c8-85b7-405e2b3285ca",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5d9dcce1-b6ee-4c6a-a93b-5431f75f936f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "05860475-0e1b-4cd9-b096-e6b547d30eac",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *pattern recognition*. Point to the three layouts on the slide and ask students to identify what matched across all of them."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "05860475-0e1b-4cd9-b096-e6b547d30eac",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5d9dcce1-b6ee-4c6a-a93b-5431f75f936f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9913aa17-afea-4fb9-82a4-177b2cdd2af2",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Many of you placed this strip across the top in very similar ways. No one told you to do that. That's because you've already learned how web apps are structured just by using them. You've been thinking like designers — even if you didn't know the technical terms yet.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9913aa17-afea-4fb9-82a4-177b2cdd2af2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5d9dcce1-b6ee-4c6a-a93b-5431f75f936f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f5b5fc2c-aaae-4e94-8f9b-4f8b43351c2c",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click to reveal the first component name: Navigation Bar. Point to where it appeared in student prototypes and on the example page. Explain that it is the menu across the top of a webpage that helps users move between pages or sections and is often referred to as a nav bar.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click to reveal the next component name: Hero Section. Point to where it appeared in student prototypes and on the example page. Explain that it is the large banner near the top that introduces the main idea, with a heading, short text, and usually a \"call to action\" button.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click to reveal the next component name: Gallery Cards. Point to where it appeared in student prototypes and on the example page. Explain that these are the boxes that group related information, each with a picture, title, and short description.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click to reveal the final component name: Footer. Point to where it appeared in student prototypes and on the example page. Explain that this is the strip at the bottom that often includes contact info or copyright details.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 1 to match the web app component to its description.",
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "This level gives an immediate read on whether students can match and apply the four component names."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f5b5fc2c-aaae-4e94-8f9b-4f8b43351c2c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5d9dcce1-b6ee-4c6a-a93b-5431f75f936f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9020fd0e-91fc-422a-afc2-bf0a1bec01f6",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students respond to the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that web apps shape our daily lives because they are interactive systems built around shared design patterns. These patterns allow us to quickly understand how to navigate, search, buy, post, and communicate. Students should understand that web apps are intentionally structured by designers to support specific actions and responses — and that this structure influences how we think, move, and make decisions online.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "As students share, reinforce these connections explicitly:\n- Web apps shape our lives because they respond to what we do — they aren't just pages, they are systems.\n- We navigate quickly because designers use shared layout patterns (like navigation at the top and grouped content).\n- When structure changes, our experience changes.\n- Behind every web app is a team of humans making decisions about how it should look, feel, and function"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9020fd0e-91fc-422a-afc2-bf0a1bec01f6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9398289d-fdd7-4aaa-a0f1-e8f38843088d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2da03ac9-f467-425a-859f-03914e81b66d",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2da03ac9-f467-425a-859f-03914e81b66d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9398289d-fdd7-4aaa-a0f1-e8f38843088d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5706f26d-3f92-4387-bf49-c932affbf79e",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *Think about an app you use every day. Who do you think made the decisions about how it looks and works?*\n- *What would you want to know about how that happened?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** This is a baseline prompt, not a comprehension check. Students don't need to get the \"right\" answer. Listen for what they already believe: some may say developers, some may say the company, some may say AI. Surface those ideas without correcting them. The goal is to capture where students are starting from — especially any assumptions that AI makes these decisions on its own — so those ideas can be revisited at the end of the unit.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary.",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5706f26d-3f92-4387-bf49-c932affbf79e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9398289d-fdd7-4aaa-a0f1-e8f38843088d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "392afb0b-e753-4e7c-aff2-7f4b62304c83",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Engage students with a visual, real-world example that makes usability differences obvious. Help them recognize that design evolves to meet user needs."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "392afb0b-e753-4e7c-aff2-7f4b62304c83",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5409306e-a9aa-412f-ab66-517f8b7e7ee4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1dc3e61b-52ec-45ee-84ea-9106f389e0c4",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Let's take a look at how web design has changed over time. Here's what Amazon.com looked like in 2000 and 2025. Take a moment to look closely at the differences in design.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1dc3e61b-52ec-45ee-84ea-9106f389e0c4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5409306e-a9aa-412f-ab66-517f8b7e7ee4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "24fce8b6-92e0-480f-b24a-7cb9cff76aa9",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which version would you rather use? Why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Listen for students noticing specific usability improvements, not just visual ones. Look for comments that highlight how modern websites feel easier to navigate, look cleaner and less cluttered, and are more accessible or efficient. Students might mention clearer menus, readable text, or AI-driven personalization that helps them find what they need faster.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students focus on looks, prompt them to think about usability. Ask, \"What makes it easier to find what you need?\" or \"How might this help different users?\" Highlight features like search bars or cleaner layouts as signs of human-centered, accessible design. You can also note that modern sites adapt to users."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "24fce8b6-92e0-480f-b24a-7cb9cff76aa9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5409306e-a9aa-412f-ab66-517f8b7e7ee4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bd7cee96-0fff-4923-aeb2-974dd844d3fe",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bd7cee96-0fff-4923-aeb2-974dd844d3fe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5409306e-a9aa-412f-ab66-517f8b7e7ee4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a137372e-737b-4114-8edb-304af3e7915d",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now that we've compared the old and new Amazon sites, let's take a closer look at the newer one. It's a great example of how modern websites are designed around people's everyday needs — clearer visuals, faster access, and more personal experiences.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a137372e-737b-4114-8edb-304af3e7915d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5409306e-a9aa-412f-ab66-517f8b7e7ee4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c5fa8006-9095-46fd-ab10-62e6b41129a6",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the features.\n- *Consider the web page features you've learned about. How do these features make the site easier to use?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should identify how common web page features such as the navigation bar, hero section, cards, and footer support user needs by making information easier to find, compare, and act on. They should recognize that these design choices reflect real user habits like scanning quickly, previewing items before deciding, and completing tasks efficiently.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students mention AI features such as \"it knows what I want,\" briefly affirm the idea and connect it to design choices that respond to user behavior. For example, you might say, \"Yes, some websites use tools that learn from what people click so they can make things easier to find.\" You can also point out that many modern sites adjust to different devices and screen sizes, which shows that designers think about how people actually use technology."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c5fa8006-9095-46fd-ab10-62e6b41129a6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5409306e-a9aa-412f-ab66-517f8b7e7ee4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "31097402-e843-467d-9272-594a652058f8",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "31097402-e843-467d-9272-594a652058f8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5409306e-a9aa-412f-ab66-517f8b7e7ee4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aaf796e0-592f-491f-98cf-2dd54d01cfb2",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aaf796e0-592f-491f-98cf-2dd54d01cfb2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5409306e-a9aa-412f-ab66-517f8b7e7ee4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "142d2369-4d96-4e4c-bf8d-38bed733d89b",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Establish the foundational concepts of user-centered design, user profiles, and user experience (UX) to prepare students for critiquing digital experiences.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the AI-Generated Design Unit Guide.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "User Experience"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "142d2369-4d96-4e4c-bf8d-38bed733d89b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9af8ff25-c671-4396-9af1-2bfdaa320c8f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8c7ad3c1-660e-4414-9ac3-4e67c7eb31e5",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, we'll be exploring human-centered design and the impact this has on the user experience. Human-centered design means making decisions based on how real people actually experience a product, not just what looks good or what's easiest to build.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8c7ad3c1-660e-4414-9ac3-4e67c7eb31e5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9af8ff25-c671-4396-9af1-2bfdaa320c8f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f6a8289a-d4d9-4ef9-aaa8-ba71629eb756",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *human-centered design*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f6a8289a-d4d9-4ef9-aaa8-ba71629eb756",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9af8ff25-c671-4396-9af1-2bfdaa320c8f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6827e731-6de7-44f1-bcd1-b5b96d653aa3",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Think about what we just saw on the modern Amazon site: it's full of visuals, easier to navigate, and sometimes even changes based on the user. Those choices came from understanding what people need and how they actually shop. That's what it means to design with the user in mind. Now connect that idea to your user profile: which of these design decisions would matter most for Jordan, Mark, or Sam?",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6827e731-6de7-44f1-bcd1-b5b96d653aa3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9af8ff25-c671-4396-9af1-2bfdaa320c8f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ef9342e5-7167-4f94-b95d-a5bb4f6fdce8",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display the Amazon screenshot with callouts showing examples of human-centered design choices (e.g., large images, clear categories, personalized recommendations). Click through the animated slide to walk through each design decision one at a time. Then have students do a quick turn-and-talk: Which decision would help your user the most, and why?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *user experience* and explain that human-centered design ensures that our users can use and enjoy our products.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain to students that they'll be stepping into the role of a UX Designer for today's activity and that UX Designers focus on creating and improving the user experience using human-centered design principles.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-video-camera\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Video:** Show the clip from the \"What is UX Design?\" video (0:05-0:48)."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ef9342e5-7167-4f94-b95d-a5bb4f6fdce8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9af8ff25-c671-4396-9af1-2bfdaa320c8f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "93bfa92f-5415-449d-bcdb-8990f5d99ba4",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Help students practice empathy by seeing apps through the eyes of different users. Reinforce that usability depends on the context and the person, not just the design itself. Provide a structured, hands-on critique activity that makes user-centered design concrete.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Place students in groups of three.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of The User's Experience activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *user profile* and explain that this is one tool UX Designers use to help them plan and improve the user experience.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "See It Like a User"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "93bfa92f-5415-449d-bcdb-8990f5d99ba4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9af8ff25-c671-4396-9af1-2bfdaa320c8f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3c8d2474-5128-4327-81b5-440a8828bf11",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Each person in your group will represent one user profile: Jordan, Mark, or Sam. Read your assigned profile carefully and think about that person's needs and frustrations. Consider what a well-designed page would look like for them.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3c8d2474-5128-4327-81b5-440a8828bf11",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9af8ff25-c671-4396-9af1-2bfdaa320c8f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6c6ff86e-ef79-4307-b39a-2142406df701",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have each student select a profile and annotate key needs in the activity guide before reviewing the app.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 2, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 4.\n- Level 1: Students read about UX Designers.\n- Level 2: Students should complete the task as their selected user, then complete Part 2: Usability Checklist in their activity guide based on that experience.\n- Level 3: Students adjust the app using the control panel to better support their selected user. Remind them they may change up to three settings and must document their reasoning in the activity guide.\n- Level 4: Students complete a Check for Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students evaluating the app from their user's perspective and supporting their checklist responses with specific details from the profile.",
        "progression_name": "UX Designers"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6c6ff86e-ef79-4307-b39a-2142406df701",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9af8ff25-c671-4396-9af1-2bfdaa320c8f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7f5b6e81-c372-4bec-8a56-5e4e34eba899",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Usability Checklist"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7f5b6e81-c372-4bec-8a56-5e4e34eba899",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9af8ff25-c671-4396-9af1-2bfdaa320c8f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9099fb92-a7ca-490f-b973-f4fcca8e55c4",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students move through the checklist quickly without explanation, prompt them to connect each decision to the user profile. Ask, \"What in the profile supports that?\" Encourage user-specific reasoning such as, \"Mark might struggle with this small text because of vision changes,\" rather than general comments like, \"The text is small.\" On Level 3, if students focus only on \"making it look better,\" ask, \"How does this change specifically help your user?\" Encourage them to consider tradeoffs and think about how a change that helps one user might create challenges for another."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 4 as a formative assessment to determine whether students can critique usability and clarity and explain how their redesign reflects user-centered design."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9099fb92-a7ca-490f-b973-f4fcca8e55c4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "9af8ff25-c671-4396-9af1-2bfdaa320c8f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0fa19cd3-37cf-4637-918b-a71afe53c060",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Consolidate the lesson by naming Human-Centered Design explicitly. Transition students from activity-based observations to formal vocabulary and concepts. Provide an early writing artifact that demonstrates growth in HCD understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What did you notice about the app that the designer might not have seen themselves? Why is that an important part of a UX Designer's job?*\n- *Whose perspective might still be missing from this design?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that designers are often too close to their own work to see what a real user experiences and that observing actual users is a core part of a UX Designer's job. Students should begin to notice that any design reflects choices about whose needs were prioritized. There's no single right answer; the goal is for students to practice asking the question, not to arrive at a definitive response.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students give vague answers to question 1 (e.g., \"the designer missed some stuff\"), press with: \"What specifically did you notice that surprised you? Would the designer have seen that from their own desk?\" For question 2, students may initially say \"I don't know\" — that's okay. Prompt with: \"Think about someone you know who uses apps differently than you do. Would this design work for them?\" This question is intentionally open-ended and will recur throughout the unit."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0fa19cd3-37cf-4637-918b-a71afe53c060",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e57dd28b-37d7-4a9c-9592-781f76eed38d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f126ebdc-fd91-415a-88b1-d9f6709a44a1",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f126ebdc-fd91-415a-88b1-d9f6709a44a1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e57dd28b-37d7-4a9c-9592-781f76eed38d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f03cfd0b-a4cf-4b67-bb27-822f68dbfb79",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f03cfd0b-a4cf-4b67-bb27-822f68dbfb79",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e57dd28b-37d7-4a9c-9592-781f76eed38d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3b782fb7-5fe8-444c-bfb0-e568abcafd13",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students use their knowledge of common web page components to predict what an AI-generated webpage will include and begin to recognize that AI generates outputs based on familiar design patterns."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3b782fb7-5fe8-444c-bfb0-e568abcafd13",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa10ae43-719a-4e27-95a0-d71e01cb7e25"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e9c0e168-c49c-43cf-beb4-7daec68271a3",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "This is an example of a prompt — it's what someone types into an AI tool to tell it what to create.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e9c0e168-c49c-43cf-beb4-7daec68271a3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa10ae43-719a-4e27-95a0-d71e01cb7e25"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "76a1804d-fe13-4c1c-9086-4185d687e4de",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What sections will likely appear?*\n- *What components do you expect?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students draw on their experience with common web page components (e.g., navigation bar, hero section, cards, footer) to predict what the AI-generated page will include. Listen for students identifying familiar structures and elements and explaining their reasoning based on patterns they've seen across websites. Students should begin to recognize that AI generates predictable layouts because it follows common design and language patterns.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students compare their predictions from the previous slide to the AI-generated web page shown. Ask a few students to share what matched their predictions, focusing on components such as navigation bars, hero sections, and content sections. Guide students to recognize that the layout and structure feel familiar because AI follows common design patterns, not because it truly understands the content. Then transition by telling students you’ll take a closer look at how AI generates these kinds of results.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Keep this moment brief and focused. The goal is not to analyze every detail, but to help students recognize that AI-generated web pages follow predictable patterns based on what they’ve seen before."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "76a1804d-fe13-4c1c-9086-4185d687e4de",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa10ae43-719a-4e27-95a0-d71e01cb7e25"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9fef436d-0cda-4b29-aa2b-9a5309c6061f",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9fef436d-0cda-4b29-aa2b-9a5309c6061f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa10ae43-719a-4e27-95a0-d71e01cb7e25"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6f6124c7-3b0f-455c-bee5-d57441e60237",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6f6124c7-3b0f-455c-bee5-d57441e60237",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa10ae43-719a-4e27-95a0-d71e01cb7e25"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "89bcaf28-f0e8-4171-ba35-cce05956a976",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students understand that AI generates responses by recognizing patterns from existing examples and that what they include in a prompt influences the result.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that AI doesn't understand code the way a human does. Instead, it predicts what to generate based on patterns it has seen in many examples. Emphasize that what students include in their prompt will influence what the AI produces. This is why the same prompt often produces similar types of web pages.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students' attention to the collection of web app examples feeding into the brain graphic. Explain that these represent the training data the AI learned from before anyone ever typed a prompt. Clarify that training data consists of real web apps created by humans and that the AI studied many examples to learn from them. Emphasize that the AI does not begin with built-in knowledge about restaurants or web design, it learns from large collections of human-created content.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Point to the repeated web app components shown on the slide and explain that the AI looks across many examples to identify what appears frequently. Highlight specific repeated features such as navigation bars at the top, red call-to-action buttons, large headlines, and menu sections with prices. Introduce the term \"patterns\" as structures or design choices that show up again and again across many sites. Reinforce that this step is about noticing similarities, not generating anything yet.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students' attention to the brain-to-website diagram and explain that this step represents generation. Clarify that after identifying patterns, the AI produces new content by predicting what usually comes next. Connect this back to the web page students saw earlier to show how those predictions result in the final layout.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain how AI uses pattern recognition and that students use this same skill to analyze layouts, debug code, and spot effectiveness.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "How AI Makes Predictions"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "89bcaf28-f0e8-4171-ba35-cce05956a976",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "89a80367-e96a-436b-829e-c605b0479ddb",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students explore the Web Lab tool and begin using AI to generate and modify their webpage, recognizing how their prompts influence the results.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students get out their copy of the AI-Generated Design Unit Guide.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Explore Web Lab and Prompting"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "89a80367-e96a-436b-829e-c605b0479ddb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d4cd0a01-7c4d-4a05-8457-89b59af763f3",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You just saw how AI generates web pages by recognizing patterns from examples. Designers often use AI tools to quickly create and test ideas like this. Now you're going to explore a tool that can generate and change your webpage for you, and start experimenting with how it works. Play around with the tool and see what you can discover. Don't worry! You can't break anything!",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d4cd0a01-7c4d-4a05-8457-89b59af763f3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8368a1d7-753a-46ed-b1a5-72ff923c5d19",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 3, Level 1 to explore the tool and select different tabs and buttons to see what they can discover.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Encourage students to select different tabs on the left-hand side, switch between Code and Preview, and try asking the AI to make a simple change to the page. Remind students not to worry about making mistakes — they can't break anything.",
        "progression_name": "Explore Web Lab",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Once students begin to slow down in their exploration, move the class into the discussion to capture what they discovered."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8368a1d7-753a-46ed-b1a5-72ff923c5d19",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "151bede6-c6de-44ed-98cb-10b1ccebe9f9",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "151bede6-c6de-44ed-98cb-10b1ccebe9f9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2bea60e3-6ca6-45ad-9474-cf1612c6ecaa",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts. Invite students to share what they discovered while exploring the tool. As students respond, briefly highlight the features they mention so all students are aware of them. If students raise questions about specific features, demonstrate them quickly without going into detail. Make sure to point out the different views, the file panel with different files and folders, and the tabs along the left-hand side which include the AI Tutor, Version History, and Backpack.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Keep this discussion focused and fast. The goal is to orient students to key features of Web Lab, not to explain how everything works."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2bea60e3-6ca6-45ad-9474-cf1612c6ecaa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "35104d43-f457-433a-bad0-71fe63078d2d",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "35104d43-f457-433a-bad0-71fe63078d2d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2fee751a-fca0-4e5d-9b94-f0b2a4426c2c",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "As you were exploring, you may have noticed that some AI changes affected the content of the page, while others changed the way it looked. Let’s take a closer look at that difference.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2fee751a-fca0-4e5d-9b94-f0b2a4426c2c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5a050b7f-c8f3-403e-95aa-73a7b7b3df8a",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide to point out that AI generates separate files for each HTML page and a CSS file.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students what differences they notice between the two versions of the webpage shown. Guide them to identify what parts seem to control the structure and content versus what changes the visual appearance. Clarify that HTML provides the structure and content of a page, while CSS controls the style and visual design. Emphasize that students will work as Web Designers and use AI tools to make changes to both structure and style.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to share a few observations before you confirm or clarify. If needed, use simple analogies such as HTML being the structure or blueprint of the page and CSS being the visual design or style. Keep the focus on noticing differences rather than explaining code."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5a050b7f-c8f3-403e-95aa-73a7b7b3df8a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a32879fe-c3ac-4a71-8fdd-e72df46376a5",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a32879fe-c3ac-4a71-8fdd-e72df46376a5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6d5691a2-1a63-4475-a415-000d875db67e",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *prompt engineering*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that AI uses the words in a prompt to predict a response based on patterns it has learned. Point out that the AI pays attention to the task, context, tone, and details in a prompt, but keep the explanation high-level. Emphasize that what students include in their prompt will directly affect what the AI generates.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students what they think the AI-generated webpage might look like from this prompt. Guide students to notice that the prompt is vague, so the AI has to make guesses about what to include.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students how the AI-generated webpage might be different with this prompt. Invite students to compare it to the previous example and identify what would likely improve. Guide students to notice that adding context and specific details helps the AI generate a more organized and useful result."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6d5691a2-1a63-4475-a415-000d875db67e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "13740154-967b-4299-ad1c-ccb65702ef96",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students use prompt engineering to generate their first AI-assisted webpage, discovering that the clarity and specificity of their instructions directly shapes the quality of what AI produces.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Level 2 to complete Levels 2 and 3.\n- Level 2: Students complete a Check for Understanding.\n- Level 3: Students complete as many AI Design Challenges as they can.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Move around the room and check that students are using AI to make changes to their page rather than editing code directly. Look for students trying different prompts, reviewing the results, and refining their page over time. Ask questions like, \"What are you trying to change?\" and \"Did the AI output match what you expected?\" Encourage students to preview their page and make adjustments based on what they see.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "AI Design Challenges",
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "13740154-967b-4299-ad1c-ccb65702ef96",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "21480526-ee2a-4bba-bbea-afda3faa635e",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "AI Design Challenges",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "This level can be used as a formative assessment of the lesson objectives, including generating a web page using AI, refining prompts to improve results, and using iterative testing to make changes to HTML and CSS."
          },
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to keep moving between challenges rather than trying to perfect one change. If students get stuck, prompt them to try a different or more specific request to the AI instead of troubleshooting on their own."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "21480526-ee2a-4bba-bbea-afda3faa635e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "747cb682-56c2-4d49-98f7-e6de196b6fe3",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "747cb682-56c2-4d49-98f7-e6de196b6fe3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f35b38e9-de52-4eff-bdc5-27104aeda43f",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *Why might AI sometimes misunderstand what we mean?*\n- *How did you decide whether to accept or change the AI's output?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reflect on their experience using AI and recognize that unclear or vague prompts can lead to unexpected results. Listen for students describing how they adjusted what they asked the AI to improve their webpage and how they decided whether to accept or change the output. Students should begin to understand that working with AI involves both giving clear instructions and making decisions about the results.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Validate student ideas and connect responses to their experience in the activity. Highlight that students improved their results by adjusting what they asked the AI and deciding whether to accept or revise the output. Reinforce that they are responsible for guiding and refining what the AI produces."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f35b38e9-de52-4eff-bdc5-27104aeda43f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "428bfd05-6979-44b4-b140-2e049c3dd8c8"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "788feda8-40d2-46aa-baff-6886154111b1",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Have students turn and talk with a partner to share their ideas. Encourage them to draw on what they noticed while prompting the AI, specifically how it generated their web page from prompts but often needed human refinement or direction. After a minute, invite a few volunteers to share highlights with the class.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *AI learns from web apps that already exist. What kinds of designs do you think it reproduces most often?*\n- *What kinds might it get wrong?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should begin to notice that if AI learns from existing websites, it will reproduce the most common design patterns most confidently — and may struggle with anything underrepresented in that data, such as designs for users with accessibility needs, non-Western layout conventions, or newer design styles. Students don't need to arrive at a complete answer here. The goal is to plant the question so it has context when it resurfaces in upcoming lessons.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "788feda8-40d2-46aa-baff-6886154111b1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "b6e9a1ed-0ca7-49d9-b64c-c996d5c72b96"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d0112596-fbb4-4b04-9729-5a541c8cd420",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show the first of two websites that varies in style (e.g., clean, cluttered, playful, serious). Ask students to jot down one first-impression word that captures their reaction to the site and one idea for who this website might be designed for. Encourage quick, instinctive responses—students shouldn't overthink.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students hesitate, prompt with: “What’s the very first word that pops into your head?” Reassure them there are no wrong answers—every impression is valid. Keep this quick and instinctive."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d0112596-fbb4-4b04-9729-5a541c8cd420",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1354b0be-f562-45f3-9daf-637442727506"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "658d95d2-4279-4d75-af28-f4cbf905a078",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "658d95d2-4279-4d75-af28-f4cbf905a078",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1354b0be-f562-45f3-9daf-637442727506"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "217b3b22-a8f4-44e9-95af-dff98db06087",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show the second website, which has an elegant, high-contrast design. Ask students to jot down one first-impression word that captures their reaction to the site and one idea for who this website might be designed for. Continue to encourage quick, instinctive responses.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Keep the pace brisk. This is about noticing differences in tone and style—not analyzing them yet."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "217b3b22-a8f4-44e9-95af-dff98db06087",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1354b0be-f562-45f3-9daf-637442727506"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "164c3405-1315-4de0-92ea-4d2f68185a84",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "164c3405-1315-4de0-92ea-4d2f68185a84",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1354b0be-f562-45f3-9daf-637442727506"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1cc59677-fcb0-41b6-8ef3-e2239152c47d",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Gather student responses for each site (use the board, sticky notes, or a digital tool). As students share their first-impression words and ideas for who each site might be for, record patterns such as repeated words, shared audiences, or clear differences between designs.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students only share one-word answers without elaboration, ask a quick follow-up like:\n\n- \"What about that site made you think it was for that audience?\"\n- \"What design choice made you think of that word?\"\n- \"Do you think most users would see it that way, or was that your personal take?\"\n\nThis keeps the activity brisk but adds depth, helping students connect instinctive impressions and audience guesses to concrete design elements (e.g., color, layout, font, imagery)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1cc59677-fcb0-41b6-8ef3-e2239152c47d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1354b0be-f562-45f3-9daf-637442727506"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bc34b66a-e9aa-4baf-87e5-b2f3a462173f",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bc34b66a-e9aa-4baf-87e5-b2f3a462173f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1354b0be-f562-45f3-9daf-637442727506"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f169f3dc-407c-42dc-99fc-387c6f72f4bd",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Notice how some of your words and audience ideas overlap while others are totally different. That shows how quickly design choices communicate — and how much style affects who a site appeals to.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f169f3dc-407c-42dc-99fc-387c6f72f4bd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1354b0be-f562-45f3-9daf-637442727506"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "17de11b2-3169-46f9-b2f4-c4afca9d8f92",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "17de11b2-3169-46f9-b2f4-c4afca9d8f92",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1354b0be-f562-45f3-9daf-637442727506"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "82e91666-d655-4eb3-ac8e-65cbcfc8fc28",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students analyze website design choices to identify strengths and weaknesses, using criteria like clarity, cohesion, and audience fit.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the AI-Generated Design Unit Guide.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Style Critique"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "82e91666-d655-4eb3-ac8e-65cbcfc8fc28",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "66170931-9e27-4aa5-aa89-af34fbb25305",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Lesson 4, Level 1. Students identify at least one strength and weakness for each web app. Remind them to think about clarity (is it easy to read?), cohesion (do the design elements feel consistent?), and audience fit (does the style match who the site is for?).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, check that they're explaining their annotations. If students write vague notes (e.g., \"bad colors\"), prompt them to be specific (e.g., \"low contrast between text and background makes it hard to read\").",
        "progression_name": "Style Critique",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Model one example first — identify a feature and label it Strength or Weakness with a short note like \"easy to read\" or \"too crowded\". This shows students that their annotations don't need to be elaborate to be effective. For advanced students, challenge them to explain how a weakness could be redesigned."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "66170931-9e27-4aa5-aa89-af34fbb25305",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fc61a3e4-c16d-4b67-bfc7-d3360685c874",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fc61a3e4-c16d-4b67-bfc7-d3360685c874",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2a4a5d52-343f-440d-b583-8c2e4e9a1e69",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What made one site's design stronger than another?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to identify specific, observable design strengths and weaknesses (e.g., readability, layout consistency, color balance) and explain how those choices affect clarity and audience fit. Use this comparison to introduce the idea of competitive analysis—learning by examining what works and what doesn’t in different designs—and to establish criteria students will apply to their own mood boards.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students respond with vague responses (\"It just looks better\"), push for specificity and prompt with focused comparisons:\n- \"Which site was easier to read, and why?\"\n- \"Which site felt more consistent in style?\"\n- \"Which site seemed to fit its audience best?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2a4a5d52-343f-440d-b583-8c2e4e9a1e69",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e230d783-7332-4ee5-b494-9be4635da629",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e230d783-7332-4ee5-b494-9be4635da629",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "508abf91-c1a4-4cb8-ab07-3082bb354e01",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students practice planning like designers by using a mood board to test visual direction before building.",
        "duration": "25",
        "name": "Building a Mood Board"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "508abf91-c1a4-4cb8-ab07-3082bb354e01",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c9757fe0-2b61-4c82-88ae-3be1d4d01e43",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You just analyzed finished designs. But designers don’t start with finished products, they start with exploring ideas.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c9757fe0-2b61-4c82-88ae-3be1d4d01e43",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9a2cdb8c-4cd9-4431-bd62-701765d25f17",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *mood board*. Explain that designers use mood boards as a tool to collect visual inspiration. Emphasize that mood boards are a familiar way to express feeling or style, not to create a finished product.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Connect mood boards to contexts students know—like Pinterest boards, vision boards, or even outfit collages. This helps students see mood boards as familiar tools for expressing style choices."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9a2cdb8c-4cd9-4431-bd62-701765d25f17",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4ca9f637-d32a-4434-949e-82c27b353fcf",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4ca9f637-d32a-4434-949e-82c27b353fcf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "582d37cb-5822-4fcd-a7a5-b08965c9b324",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students briefly observe the three mood board examples and identify one thing they have in common. Take 2–3 quick responses and highlight shared patterns like color palette, tone, or consistency. Emphasize that designers use mood boards to explore and test ideas before building.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Connect back to the Style Critique: planning with a mood board helps prevent the weak or inconsistent design choices students just identified."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "582d37cb-5822-4fcd-a7a5-b08965c9b324",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f35a4844-c4f6-4098-8805-1885b5b63f98",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f35a4844-c4f6-4098-8805-1885b5b63f98",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "46b5d44c-d36e-4d90-87ee-ef5405ff30a6",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 4, Level 2 to complete Levels 2 through 4.\n- Level 2: Students use Sketch Lab to build a mood board that communicates a clear style.\n- Level 3: Students complete a Check for Understanding.\n- Level 4: Students use their mood board to applying styling.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, check that they include all key components (colors, fonts/typography, images, and 2–3 style words). If students seem stuck or unsure how to begin, suggest they briefly visit the AI Design Help to generate ideas, then return to Sketch Lab to apply them intentionally. Encourage students who are only picking random images/colors to think about how their choices connect to the user experience and the vibe they want to communicate. Ask probing questions like: \"What feeling do you want your user to have when they see this?\" or \"Do these colors and fonts work together as a set?\"",
        "progression_name": "Mood Board Design"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "46b5d44c-d36e-4d90-87ee-ef5405ff30a6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ced20bb5-2e9b-4721-a209-2ff11a0000ae",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ced20bb5-2e9b-4721-a209-2ff11a0000ae",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dda528c2-c202-45a7-ba89-84aafc4ba509",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Apply Styles From Your Mood Board",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that mood boards don't need to be perfect or polished—they're meant to explore style direction. Suggest that students step back and ask, \"If someone else saw this board, would they instantly get the vibe I'm going for?\""
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use this activity as a formative check of how well students can apply design critique concepts to their own work. Look for evidence that students are:\n- Choosing visuals that communicate a consistent mood or theme (clarity and cohesion).\n- Making design choices that fit a user or purpose (audience fit).\n- Using vocabulary like \"color palette,\" \"contrast,\" or \"style\" when explaining their decisions."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dda528c2-c202-45a7-ba89-84aafc4ba509",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f45c1e49-37c7-4a34-84fc-cd92f5068abe",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f45c1e49-37c7-4a34-84fc-cd92f5068abe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6ca939eb-632b-4da7-b2ed-647d6f55b934",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that professional designers share work before it's finished. Emphasize that feedback helps them test whether their message is clear and whether the design works for its intended audience. Connect this back to students' mood boards—they are still drafts, not final products.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that good feedback helps someone improve their design — it points to a specific element and explains why it works or what could be stronger. Briefly model the difference between helpful and vague feedback using the examples on the slide. Emphasize that helpful feedback focuses on the design choices, not personal preference.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students walk around and review mood boards created for the same use case. Each student leaves at least one sticky note using one of the sentence frames. Remind students this is practice giving specific, focused, actionable feedback to improve a design — not judging whether they personally like it.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Circulate and listen for vague praise (“Looks good”). Pause and push for specificity by asking:\n- “What exactly communicates the style?”\n- “What design choice are you referring to?”\n- “What could they change to improve clarity or audience fit?”\n\nEncourage feedback that names a specific element (color, font, layout, imagery)."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6ca939eb-632b-4da7-b2ed-647d6f55b934",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5a6f26a2-1567-4477-9e23-d27e8b51eb0d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "90c3c012-db85-457a-b2c0-ece76d31a94d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students respond to the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide and give them about two minutes to write independently. Then invite two or three volunteers to share their thinking with the class. As students respond, prompt them to explain what they learned about analyzing designs (strengths, weaknesses, mood boards, audience fit, etc), why those insights matter when creating their own work, and how they might apply those ideas in future projects.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "90c3c012-db85-457a-b2c0-ece76d31a94d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "286637ee-7e0d-4ffc-8082-a13cb7e1ee8b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "df9b06d1-0b17-4f3e-b6bc-1b32c112c0b5",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Have students experience how colors communicate tone and emotion by categorizing palettes based on mood."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "df9b06d1-0b17-4f3e-b6bc-1b32c112c0b5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "66505817-2e37-4439-ae2d-62141aefd761"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "07b6b1c9-5aa7-489d-a025-b8152c0bec7a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Designers use color to make people feel something. Let's see what emotions these palettes create for you.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "07b6b1c9-5aa7-489d-a025-b8152c0bec7a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "66505817-2e37-4439-ae2d-62141aefd761"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "007b89f8-de79-4a82-8576-78d7f35f2344",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students label each of the color palettes with one of the categories (energetic, calming, playful, or luxury). Encourage quick, instinctive choices—there are no wrong answers.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage gut reactions and reassure students that there are no wrong answers. The point is to notice how instinctively color communicates mood."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "007b89f8-de79-4a82-8576-78d7f35f2344",
        "lesson_activity.key": "66505817-2e37-4439-ae2d-62141aefd761"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d84838f4-86dc-4da2-9f5c-720a0fd21fb9",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d84838f4-86dc-4da2-9f5c-720a0fd21fb9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "66505817-2e37-4439-ae2d-62141aefd761"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a1617582-9485-4414-8d36-2f6e8d9e3cee",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Do these palettes fit the brands shown? Why or why not?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Help students recognize that colors communicate emotion and identity — and that meaning can change based on culture, audience, or context. Emphasize how color can reinforce or challenge a brand's identity and why inclusive design considers different interpretations.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students aren't familiar with a brand, have them focus on what the colors communicate: \"What mood or message do these colors send?\" or \"Do they feel consistent with the kind of product or audience this brand might have?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a1617582-9485-4414-8d36-2f6e8d9e3cee",
        "lesson_activity.key": "66505817-2e37-4439-ae2d-62141aefd761"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "071e0f5b-76cc-46ff-b139-21a6a05f77bc",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "071e0f5b-76cc-46ff-b139-21a6a05f77bc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "66505817-2e37-4439-ae2d-62141aefd761"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "09b71a7c-5259-4329-b584-d549c3b9ba55",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Look at how powerful these colors are. Even without seeing the logo, you made strong guesses about what they meant. When we reveal the brands, sometimes your impressions match, and other times they don't. That shows how designers use color to shape how we see a brand — but color meanings can vary across cultures and accessibility needs. Even designers or AI might assume everyone sees color the same way, which isn't always true.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "09b71a7c-5259-4329-b584-d549c3b9ba55",
        "lesson_activity.key": "66505817-2e37-4439-ae2d-62141aefd761"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "637b7de4-dea8-4978-9293-6328d8aaa9d8",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day.\n"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "637b7de4-dea8-4978-9293-6328d8aaa9d8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "66505817-2e37-4439-ae2d-62141aefd761"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5942a023-3e7c-465a-8ee3-4cb530a5199e",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Have students analyze how real websites use fonts and colors to communicate brand identity and intended audience.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the professional role of a Brand Designer to frame the lesson. Explain that students will take on this role as they analyze existing brands and create their own.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Brand Analysis",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Return to this role when introducing each new activity. Remind students that they're testing ideas and refining designs, just like professionals in real branding teams."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5942a023-3e7c-465a-8ee3-4cb530a5199e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0b3a933c-3da3-474c-9f38-3305c77ea720",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0b3a933c-3da3-474c-9f38-3305c77ea720",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "360152d4-0359-4442-a938-95bddc1b6540",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Put students into groups of two.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 5, Level 1 complete Levels 1 and 2.\n- Level 1: Students read about Brand Designers.\n- Level 2: Students work with their partner and compare screenshots of websites in Sketch Lab. Students should record observations on color, typography, and then decided which audience is each brand aiming for.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Make sure students are noting both fonts and colors. Push them past surface-level comments like \"Nike uses black.\" Instead, prompt: \"What feeling does this palette communicate?\" and \"What kind of user do you think they're trying to attract?\"",
        "progression_name": "Brand Designers"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "360152d4-0359-4442-a938-95bddc1b6540",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bd50df45-c822-4fce-aafe-00f517b6e5a7",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Brand Analysis",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Check that students are justifying their claims (e.g., \"Spotify uses playful green and rounded fonts, which feels approachable for younger users\")."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bd50df45-c822-4fce-aafe-00f517b6e5a7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3c916e76-13e5-41fa-9d65-331fba3a04f5",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3c916e76-13e5-41fa-9d65-331fba3a04f5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6d390ddb-ea9d-4e02-9186-5f14ec62c001",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What patterns did you notice in how brands use fonts and colors?*\n- *How did the design choices connect to audience?*\n- *What audiences might feel left out by these design choices?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to identify common design patterns (how fonts and colors communicate tone), connect those choices to the intended audience, and consider who might feel excluded or misrepresented by the same design choices. Encourage reasoning over preference—students explain why design decisions work or don't work for different audiences. If needed, model with examples: Bold sans-serif fonts and high-contrast colors often signal energy or athleticism, while muted serif fonts and gold tones feel more elegant or luxurious. For instance, Nike's dark palette feels powerful and competitive, while Spotify's rounded fonts and bright green feel youthful and approachable."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6d390ddb-ea9d-4e02-9186-5f14ec62c001",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f150364c-5ecf-459c-ae4d-9ee96a497781",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f150364c-5ecf-459c-ae4d-9ee96a497781",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "81a9d195-ed61-47c4-81af-e2bd646a7597",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "We just analyzed how real brands use colors and fonts to communicate to specific audiences. Those design decisions don’t happen randomly — they’re organized into something called a brand kit.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "81a9d195-ed61-47c4-81af-e2bd646a7597",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "59ec211d-8bf8-4c73-8890-c808e02276b3",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "59ec211d-8bf8-4c73-8890-c808e02276b3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2a898878-fb5a-4265-8965-63f56717b198",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Have students design a mini brand identity that reflects a user persona's needs by making intentional, inclusive choices about colors and fonts.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give each student a copy of the AI-Generated Design Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that students will act as Brand Designers to create a brand kit.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *brand kit*. Point out that a strong brand kit supports clarity, consistency, and accessibility in design.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Create a Brand Kit"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2a898878-fb5a-4265-8965-63f56717b198",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3ad554bc-c02e-44a4-aa60-6721b03bb019",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "As Brand Designers, your job isn’t just to make something look good. A strong brand kit tells a clear story, stays consistent, and works for its audience. Before we build our own, let's think about what that responsibility really means.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3ad554bc-c02e-44a4-aa60-6721b03bb019",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1ada3011-400f-4ca9-bb1f-7779fd176ffb",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *If you were a marketing specialist, how would you make sure your brand kit works for many different users?*\n- *How might AI help you check your design before launching it?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to recognize that a strong brand kit must be clear, readable, and inclusive for a range of users—not just visually appealing. Help them see that designers anticipate accessibility and bias concerns before launching, and that AI tools can be used to check and refine those decisions thoughtfully.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Keep this discussion brief. The goal is awareness, not deep analysis. Let students know they’ll have access to an AI Brand Kit Helper if they want support checking accessibility or inclusivity—but they should first try to think through these considerations themselves."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1ada3011-400f-4ca9-bb1f-7779fd176ffb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b2eaa33a-b4f8-4ee0-9b1e-133952e797e9",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b2eaa33a-b4f8-4ee0-9b1e-133952e797e9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2091b56c-977b-4fa8-beff-e6722c15904a",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 5, Level 3 to complete Levels 3 and 4.\n- Level 3: Students create a mini brand kit for a fictional product and user persona\n- Level 4: Students complete a Check for Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students design, ask reflective questions: \"What mood do these colors communicate?\", \"Would these colors or fonts be easy for all users to read?\", or \"How does your design show awareness of audience or accessibility?\" Encourage students to use the AI Brand Kit Helper on Level 2 to check accessibility and inclusivity. Remind them to reflect on the AI’s suggestions—decide which ones strengthen their design and why.",
        "progression_name": "Create a Brand Kit",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students the goal isn’t just to make something that looks good—it’s to design intentionally for their persona. If students struggle, have them choose one dominant mood word and build from there. If they finish early, challenge them to test an alternative color palette and compare which better fits their audience.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use students' brand kits as a formative check of the lesson objectives. Look for evidence that students can:\n- Apply design principles to create a cohesive visual identity.\n- Explain and justify how their choices communicate tone and audience.\n- Consider accessibility and inclusivity (e.g., color contrast, readability).\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2091b56c-977b-4fa8-beff-e6722c15904a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7302df3c-5fa9-43b6-bfd7-db5c101396bc",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students the goal isn’t just to make something that looks good—it’s to design intentionally for their persona. If students struggle, have them choose one dominant mood word and build from there. If they finish early, challenge them to test an alternative color palette and compare which better fits their audience."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use students' brand kits as a formative check of the lesson objectives. Look for evidence that students can:\n- Apply design principles to create a cohesive visual identity.\n- Explain and justify how their choices communicate tone and audience.\n- Consider accessibility and inclusivity (e.g., color contrast, readability)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7302df3c-5fa9-43b6-bfd7-db5c101396bc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "97114446-def3-4f7a-80e9-5cb99f0b142b",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "97114446-def3-4f7a-80e9-5cb99f0b142b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e77466db-0ef5-46f5-97b3-932e288e57fa",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct groups to present their brand kits to a peer group with the same fictional product. Each group explains their reasoning using the provided sentence frames. Encourage students to respond to peer feedback by explaining why they made a specific choice.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for groups clearly tying color and typography choices to audience needs. If students only describe the palette, push with: \"Why does that choice matter for your persona?\"",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "As groups present, listen for evidence that students can:\n- Explain how design choices communicate tone and identity.\n- Justify how their choices serve their user persona.\n- Acknowledge accessibility or inclusivity in their reasoning."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e77466db-0ef5-46f5-97b3-932e288e57fa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c50f4332-c146-4143-a422-2fe85e6ab4b3",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c50f4332-c146-4143-a422-2fe85e6ab4b3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "323d293f-21dd-4257-a14e-33e5578d8cb1",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *How does your design avoid stereotypes or bias?*\n- *How might users from different backgrounds interpret your color and font choices?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reflect on how design choices communicate identity and values while considering inclusivity and diverse perspectives. They connect visual decisions (color, font, tone) to ethical design—recognizing that different users may interpret designs differently based on culture, experience, or ability."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "323d293f-21dd-4257-a14e-33e5578d8cb1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa016cd7-b3ef-4be3-a647-f051e1a92588"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a6c4d1b0-6f8a-4931-bcbc-d6358b176a5c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students respond to the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide. Give them time to to write independently, then hen invite a few volunteers to share. As they respond, press for connections to audience and purpose, accessibility and inclusivity, or intentional decision-making.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "As students share, highlight reflections that connect to:\n- Intentional design — making choices for a clear purpose and audience.\n- Accessibility and inclusivity — ensuring everyone can engage with the design.\n- Ethical awareness — understanding that both people and AI can carry bias into creative decisions."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a6c4d1b0-6f8a-4931-bcbc-d6358b176a5c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fff7c55c-a87e-43da-965c-aa31eac6495a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fd6b462a-639b-4505-917f-4d4d1baac3a6",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fd6b462a-639b-4505-917f-4d4d1baac3a6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fff7c55c-a87e-43da-965c-aa31eac6495a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f6ff0fe2-af66-4b45-bb4f-97b5ecf61963",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *When AI generates design suggestions, whose visual preferences might it be defaulting to?*\n- *How does that affect users who don't see themselves in those defaults?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** For the first question, students should recognize that AI design tools trained on mainstream commercial websites will tend to default toward design aesthetics that reflect the most represented users in that data — often reflecting dominant cultural, demographic, or aesthetic norms. For the second question, students should connect this to real user impact: when a brand's visual identity is generated from a narrow default, users outside that default may feel unseen, alienated, or excluded by the product.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students struggle with the first question, prompt with: \"Think about the images, color palettes, or styles AI suggested for your brand kit. Who do those choices seem designed for? Who might they not speak to?\" If the conversation stays abstract, ground it with a concrete example: a wellness brand whose AI-generated imagery only shows one body type, or a fashion brand whose color suggestions default to a narrow cultural palette."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f6ff0fe2-af66-4b45-bb4f-97b5ecf61963",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fff7c55c-a87e-43da-965c-aa31eac6495a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1b1e0912-5529-42d5-90bb-f0ed2aedbb7b",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1b1e0912-5529-42d5-90bb-f0ed2aedbb7b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fff7c55c-a87e-43da-965c-aa31eac6495a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ed725a68-0679-4e5e-8592-2e39ab1b9934",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ed725a68-0679-4e5e-8592-2e39ab1b9934",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fff7c55c-a87e-43da-965c-aa31eac6495a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1cc10781-8c2d-4c0a-872e-2a680a070d74",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students quickly recall the difference between HTML and CSS by visualizing what happens when styling is removed. They connect this idea to brand clarity and user experience — realizing that CSS gives identity and consistency to a site.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Recall that web apps are made with HTML and CSS and their roles.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Show a webpage styled with CSS. Pose the question: \"If we removed the CSS, what would be left?\" Students sketch a quick version of the same page without CSS and annotate their sketches with reasoning (e.g., \"Text still shows but without color,\" \"Images stack vertically,\" \"No clear brand identity\"). After sketching, have a few students share what they noticed. Encourage students to connect their observations to how CSS communicates tone and brand consistency.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch for misconceptions like \"nothing will show.\" Prompt with: \"Would the content still exist without CSS?\" or \"What happens to layout and colors if CSS is gone?\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *How does CSS turn a brand kit or design idea into a real web page?*\n- *What do designers need to communicate clearly when styling a page?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should connect their understanding of CSS to real-world design workflows. The goal is to get students to recognize that CSS isn't just about color or font — it's about translating design decisions into code and ensuring clear communication when turning designs into webpages."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1cc10781-8c2d-4c0a-872e-2a680a070d74",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c3f9a008-72d4-4996-b10f-35251c72ca45"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cef06385-fa47-4a70-b885-f46b5387296b",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When a designer creates a layout or brand kit, it's not automatically ready for the web. CSS is how those visual design decisions are brought to life on a webpage — translating colors, spacing, and typography into a consistent look and feel.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cef06385-fa47-4a70-b885-f46b5387296b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c3f9a008-72d4-4996-b10f-35251c72ca45"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c3632e2c-64b0-463b-904a-904d9a0a3f44",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c3632e2c-64b0-463b-904a-904d9a0a3f44",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c3f9a008-72d4-4996-b10f-35251c72ca45"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "725b8d5c-cbcb-4f21-974a-49cf08d9f7f8",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> Goal of the Activity: Establish students' role as front-end developers who translate brand kits into live, coded web pages using CSS.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that students will act as front-end developers using AI as their coding partner.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Stylesheets",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Refer back to this role throughout the activity. Remind students they're testing and refining AI-generated CSS like professionals validating a build. Remind students that AI can generate CSS syntax quickly, but it does not understand their specific brand or user needs. Their job is to define the intent and evaluate whether the output reflects it."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "725b8d5c-cbcb-4f21-974a-49cf08d9f7f8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c96e3610-ec79-4376-b1b9-33779c8442f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2d4ba127-ec91-4d52-9c56-49cb4f61393f",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2d4ba127-ec91-4d52-9c56-49cb4f61393f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c96e3610-ec79-4376-b1b9-33779c8442f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "481e6ae3-b935-4570-a071-9515ddf1d29f",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the AI-Generated Design Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *stylesheet*. Explain that CSS is the bridge between design and development: it's how the decisions made in a brand kit — like colors, fonts, and layout — become consistent code. Every time students see a website where the colors, fonts, and spacing look intentional and consistent, that's CSS at work.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the three requirements that students need to include when writing AI prompts.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which AI prompt is better? Why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should identify that Prompt #2 is better because it clearly defines the goal for the styling. They should recognize that Prompt #1 is too vague (\"look modern and professional\"), and Prompt #2 gives a specific styling goal (colors, fonts, and which parts of the page to style). Help students see the difference between a vague goal → unclear results and a specific goal → more predictable, accurate results.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which AI prompt is better? Why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should identify that Prompt #2 is better because it provides clear, specific constraints like a dark color scheme and simple font style. They should recognize that \"nice\" and \"easy to read\" in Prompt #1 are subjective, and AI may interpret those differently than the student intended. Help students understand that constraints = clear rules AI must follow, and more specific constraints lead to more predictable and consistent results.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which AI prompt is better? Why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should identify that Prompt #2 is better because it defines specific problems to avoid in the design. They should recognize that Prompt #1 is vague and doesn't prevent issues, and Prompt #2 anticipates what could go wrong (contrast, readability, spacing). Help students understand that edge cases = what to avoid, and strong prompts don't just say what to do — they also say what should not happen.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the three questions in `prompt.md`. Explain that these are the same questions a Front-End Developer asks before starting any design task."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "481e6ae3-b935-4570-a071-9515ddf1d29f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c96e3610-ec79-4376-b1b9-33779c8442f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8975d1ff-4585-4627-ab94-245e524bd268",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "The goal tells the AI what you're going for — the look, the feel, the vibe. The constraints are the rules it has to follow, like staying within your existing HTML or keeping certain colors. And edge cases are the things that could go wrong — like text that's too long, or a layout that breaks on a small screen. When you answer all three, your prompt becomes a plan, not just a request.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8975d1ff-4585-4627-ab94-245e524bd268",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c96e3610-ec79-4376-b1b9-33779c8442f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8a1d2f96-e25b-4804-9d86-aaa185336592",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that .md stands for \"markdown\" — a simple way to format plain text. Markdown files don't require special software to open or edit, which is why developers use them everywhere. They live inside the project folder, so they travel with the code and stay accessible to everyone working on the project.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk through the example `prompt.md` on screen."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8a1d2f96-e25b-4804-9d86-aaa185336592",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c96e3610-ec79-4376-b1b9-33779c8442f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "82be9cfe-64ff-4d08-802e-3c66c14940a2",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Once you've filled this in, you don't write a prompt from scratch. You give this file directly to the AI and let it do the work. The planning IS the prompt.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "82be9cfe-64ff-4d08-802e-3c66c14940a2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c96e3610-ec79-4376-b1b9-33779c8442f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3afcb441-8c93-44f5-85ad-9774ce6e1b95",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 3.\n- Level 1: Students read about Front-End Developers.\n- Level 2: Students draft a structured prompt using goal, constraints, and edge cases before prompting AI.\n- Level 3: Students use their plan to prompt AI to apply styles based on their plan.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students planned their prompt on Level 2 and use that to generate styles on Level 3.",
        "progression_name": "Front-End Developers"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3afcb441-8c93-44f5-85ad-9774ce6e1b95",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c96e3610-ec79-4376-b1b9-33779c8442f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ab2c1929-103b-4431-8df7-2d49b5537500",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Apply and Test a Stylesheet",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to \"debug like a designer.\" If the AI's output doesn't look right, they should describe why instead of just retrying the prompt."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ab2c1929-103b-4431-8df7-2d49b5537500",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c96e3610-ec79-4376-b1b9-33779c8442f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d1e0688e-40b1-42c3-ba46-6c3e07215fb8",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d1e0688e-40b1-42c3-ba46-6c3e07215fb8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c96e3610-ec79-4376-b1b9-33779c8442f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8973f823-beb5-457d-b2d5-0260f0ed1a66",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What did the AI change in your CSS — and why?*\n- *Did it make any styling choices you wouldn't have made yourself?*\n- *How do you know if its output matches your brand goals?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reflect on how AI interprets prompts and makes automated styling decisions. They consider when AI-generated CSS aligns with their intent and when it diverges. The goal is for students to see themselves as responsible reviewers, not passive users — understanding that AI can enhance efficiency but still requires human judgment for accuracy, accessibility, and design intent."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8973f823-beb5-457d-b2d5-0260f0ed1a66",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c96e3610-ec79-4376-b1b9-33779c8442f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fa726b8e-b1fa-4f8d-ab58-e0294c9fc913",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "When designers use AI tools, they don’t assume every output is correct — they check what changed, why it changed, and whether it still fits their design goals.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fa726b8e-b1fa-4f8d-ab58-e0294c9fc913",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c96e3610-ec79-4376-b1b9-33779c8442f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9eb75f7b-e630-4c51-95f4-156f04da9d5c",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> Goal of the Activity: Students demonstrate mastery by refining and experimenting with CSS using AI assistance — improving accuracy, readability, and brand consistency.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Level 4 to complete Levels 4 and 5.\n- Level 4: Students complete at least one of the challenges.\n- Level 5: Students complete a Check for Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, check that they:\n- Clearly define what they are improving before prompting AI.\n- Make one change at a time and preview the results before layering additional edits.\n- Resize the preview to check how their page behaves on smaller screens and refine the CSS if text overlaps or sections stop fitting.\n- Document what changed and why it improved the design.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Style Choice Challenge",
        "progression_name": "Style Choice Challenge"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9eb75f7b-e630-4c51-95f4-156f04da9d5c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c96e3610-ec79-4376-b1b9-33779c8442f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7cf2895d-b469-43c5-ab93-aa47a24c8bdf",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 5 to formatively assess whether students can:\n- Describe one specific styling refinement they made.\n- Explain how they used AI intentionally (including how they adjusted their prompt, if needed).\n- Connect their change to improved brand alignment and usability.\n- Distinguish between structural changes (HTML) and styling changes (CSS)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7cf2895d-b469-43c5-ab93-aa47a24c8bdf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "c96e3610-ec79-4376-b1b9-33779c8442f0"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e47d35e9-a804-4d15-b9cf-7b9c8891a5e1",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e47d35e9-a804-4d15-b9cf-7b9c8891a5e1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "29186c86-16a2-4bdd-ab82-8feff4487c7e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9e1a6a43-01e8-4f17-afb1-bda1b734d847",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> Goal: Have students activate empathy by connecting unmet technology needs with emotions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Think of a time when technology didn't meet your needs. In one word, how did it make you feel?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Help students see that unmet needs carry emotional weight (frustration, confusion, being left out) and that emotions are part of user needs."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9e1a6a43-01e8-4f17-afb1-bda1b734d847",
        "lesson_activity.key": "23072a29-d20a-4c53-93dc-8d9e52098830"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ce6cf2f8-df50-4314-a1e3-1f42cad0373c",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Those feelings are signals. If users feel frustrated or confused, something isn’t working. Our job is to design something that makes them feel more confident and supported.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ce6cf2f8-df50-4314-a1e3-1f42cad0373c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "23072a29-d20a-4c53-93dc-8d9e52098830"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bf893d98-72c2-46c8-9585-ec7b22733d00",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bf893d98-72c2-46c8-9585-ec7b22733d00",
        "lesson_activity.key": "23072a29-d20a-4c53-93dc-8d9e52098830"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3bf34182-3a70-4c2d-aa06-b45c402ba285",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> Goal: Create a user persona and use it to generate a focused, need-based problem statement.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the AI-Generated Design Unit Guide.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Persona and Problem Statement Practice",
        "progression_name": "Build a User Personas and Problem Statement"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3bf34182-3a70-4c2d-aa06-b45c402ba285",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a68e95de-4243-4dad-b743-edf64fbe0a62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "73a71285-dd6f-4632-9a61-3cd89a21738f",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today we're not jumping into features. We're not designing screens yet. We're stepping back and asking a more important question — who are we designing for? Later, when we design layouts and navigation, those decisions will come directly from a user and their needs.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "73a71285-dd6f-4632-9a61-3cd89a21738f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a68e95de-4243-4dad-b743-edf64fbe0a62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dfc03077-a991-4c53-8095-edf16c6a5262",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that strong designers define the user and the problem before building features. Briefly ask, \"Who might get left out if we only design for users like ourselves?\" Take 1–2 quick responses, then connect this to why detailed personas matter. Emphasize that skipping this step often leads to confusing or ineffective designs.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, ask students to think about an app they've stopped using and why it didn't work for them. Take a few quick responses and connect them back to unclear design, too many clicks, or not matching how users actually behave. Reinforce that this happens when designers don't deeply understand their users."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dfc03077-a991-4c53-8095-edf16c6a5262",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a68e95de-4243-4dad-b743-edf64fbe0a62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a413ca7e-9972-4d97-a2dc-d3bc48cc4b72",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a413ca7e-9972-4d97-a2dc-d3bc48cc4b72",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a68e95de-4243-4dad-b743-edf64fbe0a62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "54a0a662-084c-4f52-9ee8-5a38854cf3ba",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *problem statement*. Emphasize that a strong problem statement focuses on a user's need and why it matters, not a feature or solution.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Recall human-centered design.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "54a0a662-084c-4f52-9ee8-5a38854cf3ba",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a68e95de-4243-4dad-b743-edf64fbe0a62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6b0ef1c9-7825-4514-bb26-a5373d83defa",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "In a previous lesson, you looked at a real app and evaluated it from a user's perspective. Today you're taking that a step further — before there's even a product to evaluate, you're building a picture of who your user is and what they actually need.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6b0ef1c9-7825-4514-bb26-a5373d83defa",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a68e95de-4243-4dad-b743-edf64fbe0a62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "41354fdf-8660-465d-a820-fadb75a60281",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that before designing a solution, we need to clearly define who we're designing for. For this example scenario, invite students to help create a sample student persona. Ask for suggestions for the student's name, grade level, and 2–3 clubs they're involved in. Record responses visibly and have students write down the same details in their Unit Guide. Pause briefly to reinforce: specific details make better design decisions later.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Keep this quick. Take 2–3 suggestions and settle on one clear, realistic persona. This is a working draft, not a fully developed character. Remind them that real design decisions depend on these details."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "41354fdf-8660-465d-a820-fadb75a60281",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a68e95de-4243-4dad-b743-edf64fbe0a62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "07384a85-2a5f-4315-bc28-f2522db1a37c",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "07384a85-2a5f-4315-bc28-f2522db1a37c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a68e95de-4243-4dad-b743-edf64fbe0a62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6b3de018-3281-49a7-815c-a9507b000ff7",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that now we're stepping into the user's perspective. Read the \"Says\" and \"Thinks\" examples aloud, then ask students \"What might this student be feeling?\" and \"What might they actually do in this situation?\" Invite a few responses for each and record them visibly. Have students add the same ideas to their Unit Guide. Remind students that good problem statements come from understanding these deeper frustrations, not jumping to features.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Limit responses to 1–2 strong examples per quadrant. Focus on patterns, not listing everything the user might feel or think."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6b3de018-3281-49a7-815c-a9507b000ff7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a68e95de-4243-4dad-b743-edf64fbe0a62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b6f53090-f92f-48bc-b5d2-e3535587d304",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b6f53090-f92f-48bc-b5d2-e3535587d304",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a68e95de-4243-4dad-b743-edf64fbe0a62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e0840b5d-da1c-4052-8f56-2a3aab19cd9f",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that now we move from broad observations to focus. Based on what the user says, thinks, feels, and does, ask students: \"What is the core frustration underneath all of this?\" and \"What is the goal the user is really trying to achieve?\" Push them to be specific (not \"too many meetings\" but \"missing important information because information is scattered\"). Record one strong example of a frustration and one strong example of a goal. Have students add the same to their Unit Guide. Briefly note that this frustration and goal will become the foundation for the problem statement.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Push for specificity, then move on. Record one strong frustration and one meaningful goal that clearly connect to the user's situation."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e0840b5d-da1c-4052-8f56-2a3aab19cd9f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a68e95de-4243-4dad-b743-edf64fbe0a62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6bba7f96-c707-4697-8cf3-c8f3ea4772d6",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6bba7f96-c707-4697-8cf3-c8f3ea4772d6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a68e95de-4243-4dad-b743-edf64fbe0a62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f758bb3f-393a-4da6-a811-84372604bd27",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that now we combine the user's core frustration and meaningful goal into a clear problem statement. Remind students that a strong problem statement focuses on a need, not a specific solution (no apps, features, or tools yet). Invite a few suggestions using the template and push for clarity and specificity. Record one strong version visibly, then have students write it in their Unit Guide. Briefly note that problem statements will guide future layout and navigation decisions — what gets prioritized, simplified, or removed. For example, if a user feels overwhelmed, we might design something simple and uncluttered; if they struggle with scattered information, we might prioritize one clear dashboard.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Draft one strong class example using the template. Avoid rewriting it multiple times — refinement will happen during independent practice."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f758bb3f-393a-4da6-a811-84372604bd27",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a68e95de-4243-4dad-b743-edf64fbe0a62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4d97e7ac-d704-43e9-9df9-062c5168ec75",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4d97e7ac-d704-43e9-9df9-062c5168ec75",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a68e95de-4243-4dad-b743-edf64fbe0a62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f8edaa09-43b8-45ea-8a1e-dd870eaac564",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students use the checklist in their Unit Guide to decide which problem statement is stronger and explain why. Guide them to notice that #1 is stronger while #2 jumps to a specific solution (\"an app that sends notifications\") and does not explain the underlying need or insight.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *decomposition*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f8edaa09-43b8-45ea-8a1e-dd870eaac564",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a68e95de-4243-4dad-b743-edf64fbe0a62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9945adf4-5b36-4367-bb7f-51185ec8fad9",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Look at what you just did. You started with a complex, messy reality — a real person with a real life and real problems — and you broke it down into something specific and actionable: a problem statement you can actually design for. That process of breaking a complex problem into smaller, manageable parts is called decomposition. It's a fundamental thinking skill in design and computer science, and it's the same approach developers use when they plan code and the same approach AI uses when it tries to understand a structure before generating output.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9945adf4-5b36-4367-bb7f-51185ec8fad9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a68e95de-4243-4dad-b743-edf64fbe0a62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "167122eb-59d1-49b3-9efc-775d6b250a01",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> Goal: Students create a realistic user persona and write a clear, need-focused problem statement.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 7, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 and 2.\n- Level 1: Students choose a scenario, build a persona, and draft a problem statement.\n- Level 2: Students complete a Check for Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, listen for whether their personas feel specific and realistic (not generic like \"a student who is busy\"). Check that problem statements focus on a need, not a solution. If students write something like \"needs an app that … \", ask \"What is the actual need underneath that?\"",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Create a User Persona and Problem Statement",
        "progression_name": "User Persona and Problem Statement"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "167122eb-59d1-49b3-9efc-775d6b250a01",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a68e95de-4243-4dad-b743-edf64fbe0a62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dab912cf-9d2e-4877-b065-e404ad591735",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students get stuck, have them return to the user's frustration and goal first. A strong problem statement grows directly from those two pieces. Remind them: clarity matters more than sounding \"fancy.\""
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Student work on this level can be used as a formative assessment of the lesson objectives:\n- Create a persona representing a user type.\n- Write concise problem statement aligned to the persona."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dab912cf-9d2e-4877-b065-e404ad591735",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a68e95de-4243-4dad-b743-edf64fbe0a62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9f6abb35-06ab-4c26-b41c-9bff694a9961",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Create a User Persona and Problem Statement"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9f6abb35-06ab-4c26-b41c-9bff694a9961",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a68e95de-4243-4dad-b743-edf64fbe0a62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fe2bd43f-bde3-470f-a442-4c42768804c7",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students pair up and read their partner's problem statement aloud. Using the Problem Statement Checklist in their Unit Guide, they should give one specific piece of feedback and one suggestion for improvement. This can be shared verbally or written on a sticky note.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for feedback that directly references the Problem Statement Checklist rather than personal opinion. Encourage students to name exactly what is working or needs improvement, such as whether the statement focuses on a need instead of a solution or whether the \"because\" clause clearly explains why the need matters. If feedback is vague, prompt students with questions like \"Does this focus on a need or a solution?\" or \"Is the reason specific and meaningful?\" to push them toward more precise actionable critique.",
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Problem Statement",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Keep this fast-paced. One strong comment per partner is enough. Remind students that the goal isn’t to rewrite their partner’s statement. It’s to help them see whether their problem statement clearly focuses on a need, connects to the persona, and explains why that need matters. Encourage feedback that references the checklist directly rather than personal preference."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fe2bd43f-bde3-470f-a442-4c42768804c7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a68e95de-4243-4dad-b743-edf64fbe0a62"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "79ebfce7-74dc-4b05-9813-7538692e806b",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students respond to the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide. Give them time to write independently, then invite a few volunteers to share. As students respond, press them to explain why defining needs clearly matters and how a strong problem statement will guide future layout and design decisions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "79ebfce7-74dc-4b05-9813-7538692e806b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "04fb4b33-cdd6-4271-a5ba-d51390749296"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "14a19331-34a1-430f-a2ad-5105f30b50ea",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** For each scenario, have students write the pages the user would click in order. Start at Home and follow the connected pages until the user reaches their goal.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What do these paths tell us about how this website is organized?*\n- *What do you notice about the order of pages from general to specific?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students explain what the paths show about how the website is organized. Students should notice that users start on broad pages and move to more specific pages to reach their goal.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "14a19331-34a1-430f-a2ad-5105f30b50ea",
        "lesson_activity.key": "89de04c3-40b2-4849-aaca-cba88f886774"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "747e1147-6932-4462-beff-7db8b4ff64ed",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> Goal of Activity: Connect site mapping to real-world roles in tech and show how UX Designers plan user experiences before coding begins.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the AI-Generated Design Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Recall the role of a UX Designer and that students will use these skills in today's activity.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Intro to Site Maps and User Flow",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Invite students to name an app or website that feels easy to navigate. Ask, \"What do you think the designer did to make that happen?\" This grounds the role in tangible examples students know."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "747e1147-6932-4462-beff-7db8b4ff64ed",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a14b51c1-c5dc-4393-9f7d-0e0cf1b1d598"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d4f2d316-44f1-4e8d-a7e5-5fce203ce87b",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d4f2d316-44f1-4e8d-a7e5-5fce203ce87b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a14b51c1-c5dc-4393-9f7d-0e0cf1b1d598"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d168159e-8164-4cbe-824e-bd321d9c051e",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *site map*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define user flow. Explain that they'll map one today as part of their site map challenge.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Put students into groups of two or three.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each group a set of the Website cards.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct groups to arrange the page cards where they think users would expect to find each page, then draw arrows to show how a user could move from page to page.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ask groups why they placed pages where they did and how their layout supports user navigation. Encourage user-centered reasoning by asking questions such as, \"Why would a user expect to find Contact there?\" or \"What page would they likely click next?\"",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that the example on the slide is only one possible arrangement. There is no single correct answer. What matters is whether the organization and navigation make sense for the user."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d168159e-8164-4cbe-824e-bd321d9c051e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a14b51c1-c5dc-4393-9f7d-0e0cf1b1d598"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f05e8818-4d82-4152-90de-87846e2bdc80",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f05e8818-4d82-4152-90de-87846e2bdc80",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a14b51c1-c5dc-4393-9f7d-0e0cf1b1d598"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9ebb2312-ce41-4a79-b4d1-5a4af6c02128",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *Where did most groups put Home?*\n- *Where did most groups put Contact?*\n- *How did groups handle sections or sub-pages?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to surface shared principles of effective site maps, such as: clear hierarchy (Home at the top), consistent placement of key pages, logical grouping of related content. Capture a few criteria on the board for reference in later activities.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students mention unusual or creative structures, validate them — use these examples to discuss when breaking convention can help or hurt usability."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9ebb2312-ce41-4a79-b4d1-5a4af6c02128",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a14b51c1-c5dc-4393-9f7d-0e0cf1b1d598"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "180b8e5b-782d-45cd-bbfe-71c791da143b",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "180b8e5b-782d-45cd-bbfe-71c791da143b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a14b51c1-c5dc-4393-9f7d-0e0cf1b1d598"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "58d65c74-3e19-4898-889e-76a17d779752",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that a site map helps AI understand what pages to create, how pages connect, and how the website is organized."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "58d65c74-3e19-4898-889e-76a17d779752",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a14b51c1-c5dc-4393-9f7d-0e0cf1b1d598"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d07c751c-17a2-4f2b-b57d-69268a11c70b",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> Goal of Activity: Students will apply site map and user flow concepts to plan a simple web app, explain how its structure supports users, and use that plan to guide AI in generating a connected multi-page website.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Lesson 8, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 4.\n- Level 1: Students create a site map for one app scenario.\n- Level 2: Students trace a user flow through that site map.\n- Level 3: Students complete a Check for Understanding level.\n- Level 4: Students upload their site map and use AI to generate the web app.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** For Levels 1 and 2, check that groups are creating nested structures rather than flat lists. Prompt them with questions like, \"Would a user know where to find this page?\" and \"Does this structure help your persona reach their goal easily?\" For Level 4, prompt students with questions that build reflection and clarity: \"Does your website structure match your site map?\", \"What might you reword in your prompt to make the AI's output clearer?\", or \"If a link doesn't work, what does that tell you about how the AI interpreted your instructions?\"",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "UX Designer Challenge",
        "progression_name": "Site Map and User Flow"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d07c751c-17a2-4f2b-b57d-69268a11c70b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a14b51c1-c5dc-4393-9f7d-0e0cf1b1d598"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9366881c-b43e-4b7c-9180-7ad99824b5f0",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9366881c-b43e-4b7c-9180-7ad99824b5f0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a14b51c1-c5dc-4393-9f7d-0e0cf1b1d598"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8aa757d5-1214-4e6a-aff3-f70fdb3f50d7",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Build Your Web App with AI",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Show students how to click through the linked html pages in the navigation bar and see the URL change from index.html to challenges.html, for example. Make sure students evaluate AI’s output by testing out all of the hyperlinks in the navigation bar."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Levels 1, 2, and 4 to formatively assess student progress on lesson objectives. Check whether students can:\n- Create a site map showing page hierarchy and navigation.\n- Trace a possible user flow across a web app.\n- Articulate a site map as a clear prompt to AI.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8aa757d5-1214-4e6a-aff3-f70fdb3f50d7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a14b51c1-c5dc-4393-9f7d-0e0cf1b1d598"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "217cba01-e4f7-4c26-b6f9-e3bd98d6948f",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "217cba01-e4f7-4c26-b6f9-e3bd98d6948f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a14b51c1-c5dc-4393-9f7d-0e0cf1b1d598"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d44ff256-5f54-4b13-aadb-018a830b22a0",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *How does your site map make navigation clear and support user needs?*\n- *Why might we want to check AI’s work after we prompt it to create our site using a sitemap?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to connect human-centered design and AI collaboration. Students should:\n- identify how site maps improve navigation by organizing pages clearly, showing hierarchy, and helping users complete tasks without confusion.\n- share that AI-generated websites still need human review because AI can misinterpret the sitemap, leave out pages, or create broken or unclear navigation."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d44ff256-5f54-4b13-aadb-018a830b22a0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a14b51c1-c5dc-4393-9f7d-0e0cf1b1d598"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2cb6ca96-21e8-4eb2-82a4-69e1ed9a3b91",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2cb6ca96-21e8-4eb2-82a4-69e1ed9a3b91",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a14b51c1-c5dc-4393-9f7d-0e0cf1b1d598"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ecc76e16-8300-4916-982d-dd7e33511fc8",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *When we prompted the AI, how did we make sure it understood our site's logic?*\n- *What made your structure easier or harder for the AI to get right?*\n- *What decisions did you make today that a developer couldn't have made for you? Why does that matter?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that clear, logical structure in a site map makes it easier for AI to generate accurate output — and that when AI gets the structure wrong, it's usually because the human's organizational decisions were ambiguous or incomplete. For the third prompt, students should articulate that the structural and organizational decisions they made today — what pages to include, how they connect, what each section is for — are judgment calls that require understanding the user and the purpose of the site. These are decisions a developer could not make without that context, and they represent the core of what a UX Designer contributes to a project. Push students to be specific: not just \"I decided the layout\" but \"I decided that the About page needed to connect to the Contact page because a user who wants to learn about us probably also wants to reach us.\"\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ecc76e16-8300-4916-982d-dd7e33511fc8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "bc8c091a-ff58-474f-8359-57a4a0d380b4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c22a64c6-9423-4e72-97b9-ff33ebcbf990",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students analyze how layout and visual organization affect a user’s ability to locate information and complete a task on a website.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What would you click first to start your pizza order?*\n- *What clues on the page tell you \"this is the ordering area\"?*\n- *What’s one thing that makes it harder to locate what you need?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should notice that the layout makes it difficult to quickly locate key actions or information. They may point out competing elements, unclear starting points, or labels that do not clearly guide the ordering process. Students should begin recognizing how visual hierarchy, grouping, and clear sections help users understand where to go next.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-video-camera\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Video:** Show the \"Web Accessibility Perspectives: Clear Layout and Design\" video.\n\n**Questions to consider with the video:** \n- What made the first pizza site confusing?\n- What changed to make the improved version easier?\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that clear layout and organization help users quickly understand where to start and what to do next. They should identify specific design changes—such as clearer headings and labels, navigation bar, and consistent styling—that make the improved site easier to navigate and use.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *Where would you go first to start an order now?*\n- *What parts of the page are easier to locate?*\n- *What design choices help guide you through the steps?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should notice that the improved layout organizes the page into clear sections and highlights key actions. They may point out that steps like Find Store, Create Pizza, and Add Sides are grouped and labeled clearly, making it easier to understand where to begin and what to do next. Students should recognize that visual hierarchy, consistent layout, and grouping help users locate information quickly and complete tasks more easily."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c22a64c6-9423-4e72-97b9-ff33ebcbf990",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3b8d79cc-63d7-4dc0-9eca-0dcb4b8c2e00"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0f0f963a-6afd-4ad4-ae49-dbdc05b90ab5",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "AI tools can generate layouts that look correct but still include usability issues. Designers need to review AI-generated designs and identify problems that could make a site harder to use.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0f0f963a-6afd-4ad4-ae49-dbdc05b90ab5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3b8d79cc-63d7-4dc0-9eca-0dcb4b8c2e00"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4a01e198-7a5e-4180-b026-d35817918be3",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What could go wrong if no one fixed these designs?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that poor design can prevent users from completing tasks, cause confusion or frustration, and lead users to abandon the site. They may mention difficulty finding information, making mistakes, or being unable to use the site at all. This reinforces that accessibility issues have real consequences and that designs—whether created by humans or AI—must be reviewed and improved to ensure they are usable.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students focus only on aesthetics (e.g., “it looks messy”), prompt them to consider the impact on the user: Can the user complete the task? What might cause them to give up? Connect this to AI by reminding students that AI-generated designs can include these same issues, so designers need to review and improve them."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4a01e198-7a5e-4180-b026-d35817918be3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3b8d79cc-63d7-4dc0-9eca-0dcb4b8c2e00"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d61f7fb3-6258-4e42-b171-55d94212e2ff",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d61f7fb3-6258-4e42-b171-55d94212e2ff",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3b8d79cc-63d7-4dc0-9eca-0dcb4b8c2e00"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5da2349c-3a1d-4300-9b6b-ab2387ea71a9",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5da2349c-3a1d-4300-9b6b-ab2387ea71a9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "3b8d79cc-63d7-4dc0-9eca-0dcb4b8c2e00"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cb46e8e6-1cf5-47ba-aea5-23be69181d45",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students are introduced to the accessibility challenges that exist across the web and learn how those issues influence AI-generated designs. They explore the real-world role of the Inclusive Designer and understand why human judgment is essential in guiding AI toward more accessible outcomes.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the AI-Generated Design Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the role of Inclusive Designers.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that nearly 95% of websites have accessibility issues and highlight examples of common issues (low contrast text, missing alt text, unlabeled forms, empty links/buttons).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain how accessibility issues in web data can appear in AI-generated designs. Emphasize that AI learns patterns from existing websites, so issues in those sites can be reproduced in AI-generated outputs.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "How Accessible is the Web?",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may assume AI \"knows better\" than humans. Emphasize that AI does not judge quality—it learns patterns from existing data, including flawed or inaccessible designs."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cb46e8e6-1cf5-47ba-aea5-23be69181d45",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6828936-7e57-4ebb-8923-13f83efc67b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3a096998-9f72-4364-8026-05af2c557169",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3a096998-9f72-4364-8026-05af2c557169",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6828936-7e57-4ebb-8923-13f83efc67b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ba1e84e5-2d16-4892-b3b7-dda784596f90",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *accessibility*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 9, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 and 2.\n- Level 1: Students read about Inclusive Designers.\n- Level 2: Students explore one of the provided websites and identify potential usability or accessibility issues that could make it harder for a user to locate information or complete a task.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, prompt them with questions like:\n- \"What is the main task a user might want to complete on this page?\"\n- \"Is it easy to tell where to start?\"\n- \"Is anything hard to read, interact with, or understand?\"\n\nEncourage students to be specific about what part of the design causes the issue.",
        "progression_name": "Inclusive Designers"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ba1e84e5-2d16-4892-b3b7-dda784596f90",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6828936-7e57-4ebb-8923-13f83efc67b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "15a97075-533e-4ddd-aed2-7722c19f6274",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Accessibility Audit"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "15a97075-533e-4ddd-aed2-7722c19f6274",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6828936-7e57-4ebb-8923-13f83efc67b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6a41a4e9-2d19-4728-82fc-f4a2570c3e4e",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *What accessibility issues did you find?*\n- *Were any of the issues surprising? Why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should surface specific, concrete issues from their audits rather than general observations — not just \"the contrast is low\" but \"this text would be unreadable for a user with low vision.\" For the second prompt, students may be surprised that well-known, prestigious institutions have accessibility failures. That surprise is worth naming: even experienced design teams miss accessibility issues. If multiple groups found the same issues across different sites, highlight that pattern — it suggests these are common, systemic failures rather than one-off mistakes.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Recall that AI reproduces patterns from its training data, which means it will also reproduce accessibility issues it's been trained on. Reinforce that it's also important for human designers to watch for these patterns.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the POUR Principles as rules for making website accessible. Explain that each letter represents a type of issue designers use to evaluate websites: Perceivable, Operable, Understandable, and Robust."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6a41a4e9-2d19-4728-82fc-f4a2570c3e4e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6828936-7e57-4ebb-8923-13f83efc67b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fc802641-5841-4518-9dfd-e03ac16f8aa3",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "This is the framework Inclusive Designers use to evaluate whether a design is actually doing its job for all users.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fc802641-5841-4518-9dfd-e03ac16f8aa3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6828936-7e57-4ebb-8923-13f83efc67b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0265d24b-90c5-452d-913b-3ce99f03146e",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain what it means for something to be Perceivable on a web app.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain what it means for something to be Operable on a web app.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain what it means for something to be Understandable on a web app.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain what it means for something to be Robust on a web app."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0265d24b-90c5-452d-913b-3ce99f03146e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6828936-7e57-4ebb-8923-13f83efc67b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "845557f7-b87c-4793-90b1-b484cf68fb3f",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students learn how to use the POUR framework (Perceivable, Operable, Understandable, Robust) to describe and organize accessibility issues they observed in AI-generated layouts.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Accessibility in Design activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 9, Level 3 to complete Levels 3 through 5.\n- Level 3: Students identify at least two to three accessibility issues on the provided web app.\n- Level 4: Students apply accessibility principles to their own apps from Lesson 8\n- Level 5: Students complete a Check for Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Observe students as they identify accessibility issues and apply the POUR principles. Prompt them with questions like: “What POUR principle does this issue relate to?” or “How might this issue affect a user with this constraint?” Encourage students to be specific in their prompts, clearly describing the issue and referencing the relevant POUR principle. Push them to connect specific design elements (e.g., contrast, labels, button size) to real user challenges. Ensure students use the Accessibility Prompt Builder to generate and refine prompts that communicate issues clearly, rather than trying to fix everything at once.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "The POUR Principle",
        "progression_name": "Accessibility Issues and Fixes"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "845557f7-b87c-4793-90b1-b484cf68fb3f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6828936-7e57-4ebb-8923-13f83efc67b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dac81e6d-dafd-499b-bebd-e2f1d555a2de",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Levels 3 and 5 can serve as a formative assessment. Collect student responses to gauge their understanding of applying the POUR framework and user-centered design principles.\n"
          },
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students were absent for the previous lesson where they created site maps/apps, make sure they are aware of the AI Prompt in the instructions that will provide a site map for them. Emphasize that the goal is not necessarily to \"solve\" every issue, but to practice identifying problems and communicating them effectively through prompts. This is a key skill for working with AI and for accessibility design in general.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dac81e6d-dafd-499b-bebd-e2f1d555a2de",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e6828936-7e57-4ebb-8923-13f83efc67b6"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2f82b4de-d2f8-4331-be82-fae001199b5d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students respond to the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide. Give them time to write independently, then invite a few volunteers to share.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *Whose needs were invisible in the AI's default output?*\n- *What does that tell us about who was — and wasn't — considered when that AI was trained?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should connect the accessibility failures they identified in the audit to decisions made during AI training — specifically, that AI reproduces the patterns it learned from, including patterns of exclusion. For the second question, students should recognize that when accessibility needs are underrepresented in training data, the resulting AI outputs will reflect that gap by default. Push students to be specific: not just \"disabled users weren't considered\" but naming a particular need they encountered in the audit and tracing it back to what the training data would have had to include for AI to handle it correctly.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2f82b4de-d2f8-4331-be82-fae001199b5d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fd417ef1-11b5-466f-b064-4b37dafa8609"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c1494fe9-3302-4722-bdd6-cca685175003",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students recognize that low-fidelity wireframes are easier to change than high-fidelity designs, and begin to understand why designers plan structure before building.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which is easier to change? Why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Guide students to notice that the wireframe on the right is easier to change because it focuses only on structure, not visual details. Students should understand that designers use \"low-fidelity\" sketches to quickly test and revise layout and user flow before adding visuals or code. This reinforces that planning structure is a human decision-making step that happens before AI or developers build the final product.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c1494fe9-3302-4722-bdd6-cca685175003",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e4eb8b14-12fb-444a-bc04-2e2b989b70a1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "23059e05-9a98-4646-9748-63040a889557",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students begin creating low-fidelity wireframes by focusing on structure and layout, understanding that clear wireframes will be used to guide AI in building their webpage in the next lesson.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the AI-Generated Design Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *wireframe*. Use the blueprint analogy: just like an architect sketches a blueprint before building, designers create wireframes to plan structure and layout before adding visuals. Explain that this step keeps humans in the loop — designers decide structure and logic, while AI or developers handle styling and details later.",
        "duration": "15",
        "name": "Wireframe Sketch Sprint ",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Ask students what other kinds of jobs use \"blueprints\" or rough drafts before building the final version. This helps them connect the idea of planning structure first — a core design principle — to the broader concept of human decision-making before automation."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "23059e05-9a98-4646-9748-63040a889557",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d77b8110-2057-474e-a7c9-2bab358b0b68"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "56d557b0-e1a1-4886-b6a6-25a506bf6019",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "56d557b0-e1a1-4886-b6a6-25a506bf6019",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d77b8110-2057-474e-a7c9-2bab358b0b68"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "62fa376b-1af5-492c-beb1-e3bd31983be9",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Provide each student with a blank sheet of paper, or have students take out a sheet if they have one.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students one minute to sketch a rough home page for a fictional music playlist app. It should include basic navigation, key sections, a short paragraph, and an image gallery.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Emphasize speed and clarity over perfection. Encourage students to use shapes like boxes and lines to represent content. They should avoid details like colors, specific photos, or exact wording. This keeps attention on structure — a core idea in abstraction. If students get stuck, prompt them: \"Where would a user click first? What would they see next?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "62fa376b-1af5-492c-beb1-e3bd31983be9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d77b8110-2057-474e-a7c9-2bab358b0b68"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "adf15a3a-0768-4f38-8c36-afdf4ff8ba4a",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "adf15a3a-0768-4f38-8c36-afdf4ff8ba4a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d77b8110-2057-474e-a7c9-2bab358b0b68"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5a345287-c3e5-44fc-9379-14f1eac53bc9",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students quickly turn to a partner and compare their sketches. Prompt them to identify: what they chose to include (navigation, sections, layout) and what they left out (colors, images, detailed text).",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students get stuck, prompt: \"Did you include colors? Real images? Exact wording?\" This helps them notice they focused on structure."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5a345287-c3e5-44fc-9379-14f1eac53bc9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d77b8110-2057-474e-a7c9-2bab358b0b68"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7cb987e9-9f61-4d86-95b2-5e43f7ebba97",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7cb987e9-9f61-4d86-95b2-5e43f7ebba97",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d77b8110-2057-474e-a7c9-2bab358b0b68"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ece44f09-6e7e-4bd0-9c4f-194eeec72c40",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "If you left out things like colors, exact images, or detailed text, that's exactly right — you're thinking like a designer. In previous lessons, you made mood boards and brand kits to decide how your site should look — colors, fonts, and style. Now, you're focusing on how your page is structured — where things go and how a user moves through it. What you just created is your first wireframe — a plan for structure. So what kinds of components do low-fidelity wireframes usually include? Let's take a quick look.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ece44f09-6e7e-4bd0-9c4f-194eeec72c40",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d77b8110-2057-474e-a7c9-2bab358b0b68"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "96cdf687-8f89-4874-95a3-7599d44910b9",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Briefly introduce common building blocks of wireframes.\n- Navigation / Header → how users navigate between pages\n- Image / Video placeholders → where media will go\n- Text blocks → where information will appear\n- Buttons / inputs → how users interact\n\nEmphasize that these are simple, reusable shapes used to quickly plan layout — not final design. Keep this quick, focusing on recognition, not memorization. Let students know they can refer to these components in their Unit Guide at any time. Prompt students to consider what their sketch might be missing based on these components.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Avoid explaining every component in detail. Focus on a few core ones (navigation, sections, media, actions). If time allows, ask: \"Which of these did you already include in your sketch?\" This helps students connect the components to their own work and that they may have already used some of these in their Round 1 sketches; now we're just making them more intentional."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "96cdf687-8f89-4874-95a3-7599d44910b9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d77b8110-2057-474e-a7c9-2bab358b0b68"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "36933881-ff0d-4d19-84c3-5a51ed4abb49",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "36933881-ff0d-4d19-84c3-5a51ed4abb49",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d77b8110-2057-474e-a7c9-2bab358b0b68"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fdc5aa32-f891-4814-beab-e626d0c05c4d",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students compare the wireframe and final webpage and identify what stayed the same and what changed. Prompt them to consider which parts of the finished webpage come from structure and which come from visual design.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Give students one minute to sketch an About page for the same app. Prompt them to use the wireframe components from the previous slide to make their layout clearer and more structured. Remind them they can refer to the components in their Unit Guide if needed. Encourage students to think about how users would move between this page and their homepage — designers look for consistency across pages to improve navigation and flow.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that they are focusing on structure and layout, not visual design — this continues the practice of abstraction (ignoring details that don't affect structure) and decomposition (breaking each page into functional sections)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fdc5aa32-f891-4814-beab-e626d0c05c4d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d77b8110-2057-474e-a7c9-2bab358b0b68"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bb9fd2d1-4209-4e4e-95c6-28d87fb80f12",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bb9fd2d1-4209-4e4e-95c6-28d87fb80f12",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d77b8110-2057-474e-a7c9-2bab358b0b68"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "362a0cf7-3a7c-4972-92e0-a2a7abcab78b",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students briefly compare their own Round 1 and Round 2 sketches (or compare with a partner). Prompt them to notice what became clearer or more organized and where they used wireframe components more intentionally.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Connect students' experience from the sketch activities to choices that UX Designers make. Explain that when students sketched their pages, they were making decisions about structure and how users move through the app — not focusing on colors or visuals. Emphasize that this is exactly what designers do when creating wireframes: planning structure first so the final product works well before any styling or tools are applied.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *abstraction*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "362a0cf7-3a7c-4972-92e0-a2a7abcab78b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d77b8110-2057-474e-a7c9-2bab358b0b68"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "908bd0dc-df9b-46b6-b705-215a6711a326",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students create low-fidelity wireframes for multiple pages of an app, focusing on clear structure and how users move between pages.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Put students into groups of two.",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Wireframe an App Idea "
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "908bd0dc-df9b-46b6-b705-215a6711a326",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d77b8110-2057-474e-a7c9-2bab358b0b68"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "45862666-94c2-4c89-85d7-38e8f5adc416",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You're now the UX Designers planning your app. Your job is to decide the structure: how pages are organized and how users move through them. Make your wireframes clear and easy to understand so someone else, or AI, could build your app from them. Next class, you'll use our AI tool to build your webpage from this — so clarity matters.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "45862666-94c2-4c89-85d7-38e8f5adc416",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d77b8110-2057-474e-a7c9-2bab358b0b68"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "63a7c6e5-aed0-42ea-a8a3-38a0fd8aaea0",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 10, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 and 2.\n- Level 1: Students create two wireframes each for a chosen a web app idea in Sketch Lab.\n- Level 2: Students complete a Check for Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students have chosen a clear app idea and are creating multiple pages for it. Look for clear navigation between pages and logical connections between sections. Prompt students to think about user flow: \"How would a user move from one page to another?\" or \"What would they click first, then next?\" If groups struggle, remind them that AI can help later with visuals or content, but they are responsible for designing how the app works.",
        "progression_name": "Wireframe an App Idea"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "63a7c6e5-aed0-42ea-a8a3-38a0fd8aaea0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d77b8110-2057-474e-a7c9-2bab358b0b68"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "680e9565-15c8-4f93-94ee-76c65da3ae5d",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Emphasize clear structure and connections between pages. Consistent navigation helps users understand how the app works and reinforces pattern recognition. \n"
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use students' wireframes in Sketch Lab to formatively assess student progress on lesson objectives. Check whether students can:\n- Create a low-fidelity wireframe for their web app's pages.\n- Map a user journey across pages in their wireframes.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "680e9565-15c8-4f93-94ee-76c65da3ae5d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d77b8110-2057-474e-a7c9-2bab358b0b68"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b0061ae0-85e3-4dad-a094-ad4d01f25e87",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b0061ae0-85e3-4dad-a094-ad4d01f25e87",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d77b8110-2057-474e-a7c9-2bab358b0b68"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "577f5bcc-c787-45a3-bb01-3e53000d9224",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have pairs share their wireframes and user flow with another pair. Students place sticky-note feedback using the sentence starters. Emphasize that feedback should focus on clarity, navigation, and how easily a user can move through the app. When finished, give students a brief moment to reflect on their feedback and consider what our AI tool would need to understand their wireframes clearly.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen to the feedback being exchanged. Ensure students are giving specific feedback (e.g., “I’m not sure how a user gets from this page to the next”). Prompt students to focus on user flow and clarity, not visual details.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use peer feedback to formatively assess student progress on lesson objectives. Check whether students can:\n- Evaluate and explain how clearly a wireframe communicates user flow.\n- Give and receive peer feedback on wireframe clarity."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "577f5bcc-c787-45a3-bb01-3e53000d9224",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d77b8110-2057-474e-a7c9-2bab358b0b68"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0b2ea0f5-fc3d-4154-a850-a1e7980b7fa6",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0b2ea0f5-fc3d-4154-a850-a1e7980b7fa6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d77b8110-2057-474e-a7c9-2bab358b0b68"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a35f6492-77a7-4cf9-9d8b-78322a260f6d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students respond to the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide. Give them time to write independently, then invite a few volunteers to share. As students respond, press them to explain why sketching first improves clarity and structure, not just that it is faster or easier, and guide them to connect their answers to planning layout, user flow, and making their designs easier for others to understand.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students give vague answers (e.g., “it’s easier”), prompt with questions like, “What specifically becomes easier?” or “How does sketching help someone else understand your design?” to push for more precise reasoning.\n"
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a35f6492-77a7-4cf9-9d8b-78322a260f6d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6987f845-0307-46f4-95e1-cb23334e7b13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "841f53d3-0b86-4dee-a77b-8e7eded06211",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "841f53d3-0b86-4dee-a77b-8e7eded06211",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6987f845-0307-46f4-95e1-cb23334e7b13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6eb4d802-da29-47dd-a76c-7c6cbd5938ae",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6eb4d802-da29-47dd-a76c-7c6cbd5938ae",
        "lesson_activity.key": "6987f845-0307-46f4-95e1-cb23334e7b13"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4434fab1-54f0-4982-a9c6-b6b52b788bdc",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students recognize that combining structure (a map) with instructions (directions) leads to clearer results—introducing the idea that AI works best when given both a wireframe and a prompt.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students quickly vote (by raising hands or turning to a partner) for A, B, or C. After voting, briefly highlight that a map provides structure while directions explain what to do, and that using both together leads to clearer understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Point to the wireframe and written description on the slide and explain how each provides different information. Emphasize that the wireframe shows the structure and layout of the page, while the written description explains what each part is for and what content should go there. Then connect this to the lesson by explaining that AI works best when it has both—structure from the wireframe and instructions from the prompt.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4434fab1-54f0-4982-a9c6-b6b52b788bdc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "816f3e71-d57c-43cc-9b64-6a3fe2407ce9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d1ce5209-880a-4fd6-9cb5-c12db6ffac4d",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students understand that AI interprets wireframes by recognizing patterns and may misinterpret unclear or ambiguous designs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the AI-Generated Design Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Point to each image from left to right and guide students through the process. Start with the wireframe and ask what they notice (such as sections, images, or navigation). Then explain that the AI scans the layout for patterns like spacing, grouping, and labels. Next, show how the AI uses those patterns to infer components and structure (such as headers, sections, or cards). Finally, explain that the AI generates code based on those predictions. Emphasize that at each step, the AI is making interpretations—not perfectly understanding the design.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "How AI Interprets Wireframes",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may assume AI \"understands\" their design the same way they do. Emphasize that at each step—scanning patterns, inferring structure, and generating code—the AI is making educated guesses. This is why unclear layouts or missing details can lead to unexpected results. Keep this section brief and move quickly into the activity.\n\nKeep this section brief—focus on helping students recognize that AI interprets and may misinterpret wireframes, then move quickly into the activity."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d1ce5209-880a-4fd6-9cb5-c12db6ffac4d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5cc5c74a-4e33-481b-89b0-4d9c927a57bb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "82aa7390-9d5e-4776-b6e1-e3f36204c549",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "82aa7390-9d5e-4776-b6e1-e3f36204c549",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5cc5c74a-4e33-481b-89b0-4d9c927a57bb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3d06d653-56a3-45b3-9fb0-38f9b2ed26e2",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Point to the wireframe example and highlight specific problem areas (unclear shapes, spacing, or overlapping elements). Explain that AI relies on recognizable patterns, so when these elements are unclear or inconsistent, the AI may interpret them incorrectly. Emphasize that when structure, grouping, or hierarchy isn’t clearly defined, the AI will make its best guess, which may not match the intended design. Connect this to prompting by noting that clearer descriptions and constraints help guide the AI toward a more accurate layout.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may assume AI will understand their design exactly as they do. Use this example to highlight that AI makes guesses based on patterns. Later, if students say the AI \"got it wrong,\" prompt them to ask, \"What might the AI have been confused by?\" and connect mismatches back to unclear shapes, spacing, or missing details in their wireframe or prompt."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3d06d653-56a3-45b3-9fb0-38f9b2ed26e2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5cc5c74a-4e33-481b-89b0-4d9c927a57bb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "aa186eb8-42c0-4b59-8f5d-0181dc335b84",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "aa186eb8-42c0-4b59-8f5d-0181dc335b84",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5cc5c74a-4e33-481b-89b0-4d9c927a57bb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "284914f4-f387-4c83-9f33-96146032fbb8",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What's one thing in this wireframe that could be interpreted in different ways?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students identify parts of the wireframe that could be interpreted in different ways and may disagree on what certain elements represent (for example, whether something is a button, logo, image, or card). Through sharing different responses, they recognize that people interpret the same visual differently. Use that moment to highlight that this same ambiguity applies to AI—it does not \"know\" the correct answer and instead makes its best guess based on patterns, which can lead to mismatches with the intended design."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "284914f4-f387-4c83-9f33-96146032fbb8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5cc5c74a-4e33-481b-89b0-4d9c927a57bb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1c298ca4-eb77-483c-b97c-edfa59876c5b",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students generate a layout from a wireframe using a planned prompt, then compare the AI output to their design to identify matches and mismatches.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the From Wireframes to Layouts activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 11, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 and 2.\n- Level 1: Explain that they will use their wireframe and planned prompt to generate a layout, then compare the AI output to their design to identify matches and mismatches.\n- Level 2: Students complete a Check for Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Observe how students reason through the comparison. Ask guiding questions such as:\n- \"What part of your layout matches your wireframe most closely?\"\n- \"What doesn't match, and how is it different from your design?\"\n- \"Why do you think the AI interpreted that part this way?\"\n- \"What could you change in your prompt to improve that?\"",
        "duration": "20",
        "name": "Generate Using Wireframes",
        "progression_name": "Generate From a Wireframe"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1c298ca4-eb77-483c-b97c-edfa59876c5b",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5cc5c74a-4e33-481b-89b0-4d9c927a57bb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ed08db48-f3e6-40a8-81f1-093f71598511",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "- If students didn't finish all four pages of their wireframe last class, reassure them that one completed page is enough to continue.\n- If students do not have a wireframe, have them use the Sample Wireframe provided in the level's panel files.\n- If the AI output doesn't match their design, encourage students to revise their prompt with more specific layout details or constraints."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use student responses in the activity guide and their work in Level 1 to formatively assess whether students can generate layouts from a wireframe, evaluate how well the AI output aligns with their design, and refine their prompts to improve that alignment."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ed08db48-f3e6-40a8-81f1-093f71598511",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5cc5c74a-4e33-481b-89b0-4d9c927a57bb"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1ba43c2e-a088-431c-97eb-cfd38e5f359f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students respond to the Question of the Day in their Unit Guide. Give them time to write independently, then invite 1–2 students to share. As students respond, prompt them to explain how they used their wireframe and prompt to guide the AI, and how they took responsibility for evaluating and refining the output when it didn’t match their design."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1ba43c2e-a088-431c-97eb-cfd38e5f359f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "97c3c98d-c0de-4928-aae2-63003824c35a"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "09295084-9bd4-4f74-b1e9-ae47b4b846fd",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students see firsthand how layouts change across screen sizes and start noticing breakpoints and issues.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What changes? What breaks?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that the layout stays fixed instead of adapting, causing content to overflow and images to distort.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What changes? What breaks?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that the layout stays fixed instead of adapting, causing content to overflow.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What changes? What breaks?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that the layout stays fixed instead of adapting, causing text to shrink and be unreadable.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What changes? What doesn’t break?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that the layout adapts to different screen sizes, with content resizing and rearranging so everything stays visible and easy to use.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "09295084-9bd4-4f74-b1e9-ae47b4b846fd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ae02d398-c21a-483e-bfdd-09b5bb04af70"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0c002160-d1d8-4e02-a750-7a04cd87d663",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students test and refine their AI-generated layouts for responsiveness across devices, using checklists and peer review to spot and fix issues.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the AI-Generated Design Unit Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *responsiveness*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Read the key stats aloud to students. Then, read what to consider when creating a responsive, user friendly website.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *If AI learns from old, non-responsive websites, how might that affect the layouts it creates today?*\n- *How could human designers help guide AI to create more mobile-friendly or flexible layouts?*\n- *What can AI not do on its own when building responsive websites?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that AI's outputs are shaped by its training data and prompts, which can lead to non-responsive designs. They understand that AI cannot test layouts across devices or guarantee usability, so humans must test, identify issues, and decide how to refine the site rather than relying on AI alone.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-video-camera\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Video:** Show the \"A Day in the Life of a Software Test Engineer at GM\" video."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0c002160-d1d8-4e02-a750-7a04cd87d663",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a341473e-c3e6-4b00-8dad-04f42d61c99d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "11884518-9c87-490f-8b1d-497ad0cf1d7a",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today, you'll get to practice thinking like a Test Engineer. You'll look at an AI-generated webpage, test it for responsiveness, and then use what you find to guide the AI toward better, more responsive design choices.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "11884518-9c87-490f-8b1d-497ad0cf1d7a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a341473e-c3e6-4b00-8dad-04f42d61c99d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ce2a9c95-0b2c-4c6d-84e5-2428f67c68a0",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Designing for Responsiveness activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 12, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 and 2.\n- Level 1: Students test how the site behaves on different screen sizes using the Responsiveness Checklist, identify issues, and use AI to fix problems.\n- Level 2: Students complete a Check for Understanding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, check that they:\n- Name specific issues (not just \"it looks bad\")\n- Focus on one issue at a time\n- Test changes by resizing after each fix\n- Explain how their fix improves usability on smaller screens",
        "progression_name": "Web App Responsiveness"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ce2a9c95-0b2c-4c6d-84e5-2428f67c68a0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a341473e-c3e6-4b00-8dad-04f42d61c99d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e0c423cc-eec9-420b-be10-63c12abca060",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Check for Understanding",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students get stuck, redirect them to a small, specific issue (e.g., navigation, text, images) instead of trying to fix everything at once."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Level 2 can serve as a formative assessment. Collect student responses to gauge their understanding of responsiveness in design."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e0c423cc-eec9-420b-be10-63c12abca060",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a341473e-c3e6-4b00-8dad-04f42d61c99d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a12e3985-afdb-4837-8af7-f285cc663c3e",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a12e3985-afdb-4837-8af7-f285cc663c3e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a341473e-c3e6-4b00-8dad-04f42d61c99d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "80174270-576d-44c0-95b0-96104cdaa20d",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Why should we design websites that work well on all devices and screen sizes?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that responsive design is more inclusive for all users. Push students to think about who is excluded when websites are not designed responsively, e.g. a website that only displays content well for desktops."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "80174270-576d-44c0-95b0-96104cdaa20d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "a341473e-c3e6-4b00-8dad-04f42d61c99d"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e45814fb-b9a3-4811-b4f0-7eaf970ca637",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e45814fb-b9a3-4811-b4f0-7eaf970ca637",
        "lesson_activity.key": "40387f4f-233f-45a7-909e-6b3076890131"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "77794e7a-fd7c-43ac-8505-5eda2d8a7077",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain what a digital magazine is.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *If you could create a magazine about anything, what would it be? Who would read it?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students connect personal interests to an audience or community, priming them to think about purpose and audience in their digital magazine project.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "77794e7a-fd7c-43ac-8505-5eda2d8a7077",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa9a4d79-3dd4-46ef-bfce-56b06b84871b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "01d16791-3307-4052-9640-eced4c4c445e",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Digital Magazine Project Planning Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the first part of the project requirements.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to read the rubric on the last two pages of the project planning guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What do you notice about the project requirements and rubric? What does success look like for this project?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students share what they notice about the project requirements and rubric and identify the components to implement in their program.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the milestone structure of the project. Explain that the Digital Magazine Project is divided into three milestones, each with its own goals and deliverables. Use the visual roadmap to preview the journey students will take — from defining their magazine concept to showcasing their final product.",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that milestones help them manage time and track progress, just like in real design and development projects. Encourage them to think of each milestone as a \"checkpoint\" to reflect on what's working, what needs improvement, and how their design aligns with their user and purpose."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "01d16791-3307-4052-9640-eced4c4c445e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa9a4d79-3dd4-46ef-bfce-56b06b84871b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2a1eec47-7bf7-49b9-bf3f-8709ec091076",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2a1eec47-7bf7-49b9-bf3f-8709ec091076",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa9a4d79-3dd4-46ef-bfce-56b06b84871b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a54e66ff-832d-4d7a-9194-e07a0af3a787",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that today's focus is Milestone 1: Define Your Magazine, where students plan their magazine's concept, audience, and design direction. Direct students to open their Digital Magazine Project Guide and locate the Milestone 1 section. Have them read through the Goals and Deliverables so they understand what needs to be completed by the end of this milestone.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to highlight or note parts of the deliverables that feel most exciting or challenging. This helps you gauge engagement and plan support for different groups."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a54e66ff-832d-4d7a-9194-e07a0af3a787",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa9a4d79-3dd4-46ef-bfce-56b06b84871b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cdbc385d-6f39-4a76-b595-fec6334cda59",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct student to Lesson 13, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 6.\n- Level 1: Students read an overview of Milestone 1.\n- Level 2: Students plan the theme and main interaction of their digital magazine.\n- Level 3: Students create a brand kit for their digital magazine.\n- Level 4: Students create a site map for their digital magazine.\n- Level 5: Students create wireframes for at least two pages of their digital magazine.\n- Level 6: Students respond to the Milestone 1 Reflection question.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students work, ask guiding questions such as:\n- \"How would you describe your magazine's main audience in one sentence?\"\n- \"What kind of stories or visuals would fit your theme?\"\n- \"How might your design choices make your magazine feel more cohesive?\"\n\nUse these quick check-ins to confirm that students are connecting their creative choices to a clear audience and purpose.",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 1: Define"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cdbc385d-6f39-4a76-b595-fec6334cda59",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa9a4d79-3dd4-46ef-bfce-56b06b84871b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "62d348d8-8cf9-4a2a-9b8a-f562744f2463",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Milestone 1 Reflection",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Set expectations for pacing — Milestone 1 can typically be finished by the end of today's class or early next class. Encourage students to focus first on clarity of idea rather than polish; they'll refine visuals and layouts later in the project."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "62d348d8-8cf9-4a2a-9b8a-f562744f2463",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa9a4d79-3dd4-46ef-bfce-56b06b84871b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4d351343-9de1-47fb-8c6c-74a575c38db4",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Milestone 1"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4d351343-9de1-47fb-8c6c-74a575c38db4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa9a4d79-3dd4-46ef-bfce-56b06b84871b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ca791e62-f897-4012-accb-2d1ee4f8547a",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Who is your magazine for, and what do you want them to feel when they read it? What one design decision have you already made that reflects that?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should be able to name a specific user — not a generic one like \"teenagers\" or \"people who like sports,\" but someone with a particular context, need, or perspective that is shaping their design choices. For the second part, students should connect their user definition to at least one concrete decision they've already made, such as a color palette choice, a content section they decided to include, or a layout approach — and be able to explain why that decision serves their user specifically.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ca791e62-f897-4012-accb-2d1ee4f8547a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa9a4d79-3dd4-46ef-bfce-56b06b84871b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6cdaea00-9d86-4333-b9dc-aad0f3f0a7f3",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What's one element you're excited to see come to life from your design?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reconnect with their design vision and build enthusiasm to begin the hands-on coding phase. This primes them to think intentionally about how their design ideas translate into real HTML/CSS builds today.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6cdaea00-9d86-4333-b9dc-aad0f3f0a7f3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "fa9a4d79-3dd4-46ef-bfce-56b06b84871b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "87ca75f4-9345-406c-8799-cb4d242d42c9",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What's one element you're excited to see come to life from your design?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reconnect with their design vision and build enthusiasm to begin the hands-on coding phase. This primes them to think intentionally about how their design ideas translate into real HTML/CSS builds today.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "87ca75f4-9345-406c-8799-cb4d242d42c9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ce0571db-6c55-4805-b665-3a08c5e9f612"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "82def46b-d238-4186-b410-5944a670a406",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the tasks to complete for Day 2 of the project. Explain that today students will focus on building their digital magazine and getting peer feedback.",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students not to aim for perfection at first—just to get their layout functioning, then improve it through feedback and iteration."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "82def46b-d238-4186-b410-5944a670a406",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ce0571db-6c55-4805-b665-3a08c5e9f612"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0819d1aa-2e99-4cbe-92fa-db74f0dc82ce",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 13, Level 7 to complete Levels 7 through 9.\n- Level 7: Students read an overview of Milestone 2.\n- Level 8: Students import their planning resources from Milestone 1 from their Backpack and create the pages for their digital magazine.\n- Level 9: Students respond to the Milestone 2 Reflection question.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ensure students are on task and progressing toward a complete first draft. Ask check-in questions like:\n- \"What part of your magazine are you starting with?\"\n- \"What feedback from your plan or Milestone 1 are you using as you build?\"\n- \"Have you tested your page to make sure it behaves the way you expect?\"",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 2: Build"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0819d1aa-2e99-4cbe-92fa-db74f0dc82ce",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ce0571db-6c55-4805-b665-3a08c5e9f612"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fc80c524-f720-450d-ab48-3a97167cde0c",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Milestone 2 Reflection"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fc80c524-f720-450d-ab48-3a97167cde0c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ce0571db-6c55-4805-b665-3a08c5e9f612"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e813196c-4355-417b-8b30-f19e7370da7f",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain the TAG Peer Review process and model what thoughtful feedback looks like. Have students pair up or form small groups and TAG one to two peers' projects. They can leave feedback on sticky notes (digital or paper) directly at their peer's workspace or comment within the project guide if using shared docs.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may be at different points when it's time for peer review. Reassure them that work doesn't need to be complete to be reviewed — feedback during the process helps improve the final product. Reinforce the mindset that design and development are iterative, and peer feedback offers new perspectives and guidance. If time allows, invite a few students to share one piece of feedback they received that will guide their next steps."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e813196c-4355-417b-8b30-f19e7370da7f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ce0571db-6c55-4805-b665-3a08c5e9f612"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "57f64b42-21ae-4b5a-89bf-ccdfc2fc58ba",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "57f64b42-21ae-4b5a-89bf-ccdfc2fc58ba",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ce0571db-6c55-4805-b665-3a08c5e9f612"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3bca3854-f066-4ea8-a84f-26d0e903d859",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What was one moment today where the AI gave you something unexpected? What did you decide to do about it?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should describe a specific moment of AI output that surprised, disappointed, or confused them — not a general feeling that \"the AI didn't get it right,\" but a concrete example of what the AI produced and why it didn't match their intent. For the second part, students should articulate a deliberate decision: they either refined their prompt and tried again, accepted the output and explained why it actually worked, or manually adjusted something and explained what they changed. Push students who say they just accepted the AI's output without reflection — ask them whether the result actually matched their user and their design intention from Day 1.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3bca3854-f066-4ea8-a84f-26d0e903d859",
        "lesson_activity.key": "ce0571db-6c55-4805-b665-3a08c5e9f612"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ff54d47e-d2f2-4365-901d-87a4d19d20b4",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What challenge do you still need to solve before your digital magazine is ready to share?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students identify one specific challenge or improvement area that will guide their focus during today's work.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ff54d47e-d2f2-4365-901d-87a4d19d20b4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f2a59972-b9d7-464e-a0e1-c5e0c9593331"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "386c8601-1cc0-40e0-ad9d-4317dc5095c9",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the tasks to complete for Day 3 of the project. Explain that today is focused on polishing their site using feedback from peers, teachers, and their own self-assessment.",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Frame today as a celebration of their work and growth as designers. Say something like: \"Today's your chance to put the finishing touches on your magazine and share what you've built. Every project is unique — focus on making sure yours reflects your audience, your ideas, and your effort.\" If you're doing a showcase, preview how students will present (e.g., rotating stations, quick pitches, or feedback slips)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "386c8601-1cc0-40e0-ad9d-4317dc5095c9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f2a59972-b9d7-464e-a0e1-c5e0c9593331"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "565bed9c-9f42-4401-8c47-bc02a23965d2",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "565bed9c-9f42-4401-8c47-bc02a23965d2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f2a59972-b9d7-464e-a0e1-c5e0c9593331"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f9b4cf30-de8c-4ed9-a3cb-be3fc5e942e1",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to the last two pages of their Digital Magazine Project Guide, where the project rubric is located. Have students review each category carefully and mark where they believe their project currently falls. Encourage honesty — the goal is to identify strengths and areas for continued growth as intentional designers.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students are engaging thoughtfully with each rubric criterion, not rushing to completion.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that self-assessment isn't just about scoring their project — it's a professional habit that helps them take ownership of their design choices. Say something like: \"Designers constantly evaluate their own work to make sure it aligns with their goals, audience, and purpose. This rubric helps you see where you've grown and what you can still refine.\"\n\nIf time allows, have students highlight one category they feel especially proud of and one they'd like to improve next time."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f9b4cf30-de8c-4ed9-a3cb-be3fc5e942e1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f2a59972-b9d7-464e-a0e1-c5e0c9593331"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4973236c-3c1d-4036-98a5-b9fbe86c4f13",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4973236c-3c1d-4036-98a5-b9fbe86c4f13",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f2a59972-b9d7-464e-a0e1-c5e0c9593331"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4b204711-ae28-43e5-b3ba-a13b90e5a084",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 13, Level 10 to complete Levels 10 through 12.\n- Level 10: Students read an overview of Milestone 3.\n- Level 11: Students finalize their digital magazine.\n- Level 12: Students respond to the Final Reflection questions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ensure students are on task. Remind students to comment their code with meaningful descriptions. Have students review their rubric and prioritize work on any rows where they do not yet show extensive evidence.",
        "progression_name": "Milestone 3: Finalize"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4b204711-ae28-43e5-b3ba-a13b90e5a084",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f2a59972-b9d7-464e-a0e1-c5e0c9593331"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "63452eec-ca7f-4bd8-97ea-ae2a5b7b1345",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Final Reflection",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that professional designers always prioritize fixes before a release. Say something like: \"You don't need to fix everything today — focus on what will have the biggest impact for your user. Iteration is about making smart choices with the time you have.\"\n\nIf time allows at the end, invite students to do a brief pair preview, where they open a peer's site and give one final piece of positive or constructive feedback."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "63452eec-ca7f-4bd8-97ea-ae2a5b7b1345",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f2a59972-b9d7-464e-a0e1-c5e0c9593331"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "91651fc1-4ece-4cd8-914a-81e02c6f48f3",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "91651fc1-4ece-4cd8-914a-81e02c6f48f3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f2a59972-b9d7-464e-a0e1-c5e0c9593331"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4e730388-95ef-442f-a39b-868326e7edb6",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Distribute several sticky notes to each student for the gallery walk.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students begin the Gallery Walk by displaying their digital magazines on their computers, then moving around the room to explore classmates' projects. As they visit each one, students should use their sticky notes to leave feedback, kudos, or questions following the TAG approach (Tell, Ask, Give). Encourage them to engage with a range of projects—not just those of close friends.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Move around the room to observe how students are engaging with the displayed projects. Encourage students to leave a note about what they find interesting or insightful about their peers' work. Ensure that students are thoughtfully engaging with a variety of projects, not just those of their closest peers.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Frame the Gallery Walk as a celebration of learning and creativity, not a critique. Say something like: \"This is your chance to see how everyone's ideas came to life in different ways. You might even get inspired by someone else's approach!\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4e730388-95ef-442f-a39b-868326e7edb6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f2a59972-b9d7-464e-a0e1-c5e0c9593331"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "389c1583-feb9-409c-8966-4c63687ce628",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Does your final magazine actually serve the user you defined on Day 1, and how do you know?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should make a specific, evidence-based claim about whether their magazine serves their intended user — not \"yes I think it looks good\" but \"yes, because I designed the navigation so that a first-time reader can find the featured article immediately\" or \"not entirely, because the font size is still small on mobile and my user mostly reads on their phone.\" Push students who give purely positive self-assessments to identify at least one thing they would change if they had more time and why. Push students who are overly critical to name something that genuinely works for their user.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "389c1583-feb9-409c-8966-4c63687ce628",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f2a59972-b9d7-464e-a0e1-c5e0c9593331"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c3e49134-9fb6-4735-a599-27958772c0c3",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "End of Unit Assessment"
